-- V467: Seed 130 Class 10 Science Mock Tests (104 Real-Format 80M Papers + 26 Chapter-Wise 25M Mocks)

DO $$
DECLARE
    v_class10_id UUID;
BEGIN
    SELECT id INTO v_class10_id FROM learn_exam_types WHERE slug IN ('class-10', 'cbse-class-10') LIMIT 1;
    IF v_class10_id IS NULL THEN
        SELECT id INTO v_class10_id FROM learn_exam_types WHERE name ILIKE '%Class 10%' LIMIT 1;
    END IF;

    IF v_class10_id IS NOT NULL THEN
        -- Clean legacy 50-mark placeholder on Class 10
        DELETE FROM learn_test_attempts WHERE mock_test_id = 'a1010101-0000-0000-0000-000000000010';
        DELETE FROM learn_test_questions WHERE mock_test_id = 'a1010101-0000-0000-0000-000000000010';
        DELETE FROM learn_mock_tests WHERE id = 'a1010101-0000-0000-0000-000000000010';

        DELETE FROM learn_test_questions WHERE mock_test_id IN (SELECT id FROM learn_mock_tests WHERE id::text LIKE '10010000-%');
        DELETE FROM learn_mock_tests WHERE id::text LIKE '10010000-%';

        ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

Reason (R): Silver reacts with hydrogen sulphide present in air to form a coating of black silver sulphide (Ag₂S).
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Chemical Reactions and Equations', 1, 'Corrosion of silver forms black Ag₂S.', NULL, NULL, 'Corrosion of silver forms black Ag₂S.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000a', 'Assertion (A): Calcium hydroxide solution is used for whitewashing walls.
Reason (R): Calcium hydroxide reacts slowly with atmospheric CO₂ to form a shiny layer of calcium carbonate.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Chemical Reactions and Equations', 1, 'Ca(OH)₂ + CO₂ -> CaCO₃ + H₂O.', NULL, NULL, 'Ca(OH)₂ + CO₂ -> CaCO₃ + H₂O.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000b', 'Write the balanced chemical equation for the thermal decomposition of lead nitrate crystals and state one observation.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Chemical Reactions and Equations', 2, '2Pb(NO₃)₂(s) --Heat--> 2PbO(s) + 4NO₂(g) + O₂(g). Observation: Brown fumes of nitrogen dioxide (NO₂) gas are evolved and yellow residue of PbO remains.', 'Balanced equation (1M) + Brown fumes observation (1M)', NULL, '2Pb(NO₃)₂(s) --Heat--> 2PbO(s) + 4NO₂(g) + O₂(g). Observation: Brown fumes of nitrogen dioxide (NO₂) gas are evolved and yellow residue of PbO remains.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000c', 'Distinguish between oxidation and reduction in terms of electron transfer or oxygen/hydrogen with one example each.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Chemical Reactions and Equations', 2, 'Oxidation is gain of oxygen / loss of hydrogen or electrons (e.g., 2Cu + O₂ -> 2CuO). Reduction is loss of oxygen / gain of hydrogen or electrons (e.g., CuO + H₂ -> Cu + H₂O).', 'Definitions (1M) + Balanced examples (1M)', NULL, 'Oxidation is gain of oxygen / loss of hydrogen or electrons (e.g., 2Cu + O₂ -> 2CuO). Reduction is loss of oxygen / gain of hydrogen or electrons (e.g., CuO + H₂ -> Cu + H₂O).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000d', 'Why should a magnesium ribbon be cleaned with sandpaper before burning in air?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Chemical Reactions and Equations', 2, 'Magnesium ribbon gets coated with a protective layer of basic magnesium oxide/carbonate due to reaction with moist air. Rubbing with sandpaper removes this oxide layer so magnesium burns smoothly.', 'Oxide layer explanation (1M) + Purpose (1M)', NULL, 'Magnesium ribbon gets coated with a protective layer of basic magnesium oxide/carbonate due to reaction with moist air. Rubbing with sandpaper removes this oxide layer so magnesium burns smoothly.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000e', 'A reddish-brown metal powder A turns black on heating in a china dish. When hydrogen gas is passed over the heated black substance B, the original reddish-brown colour is restored. Identify A and B and write balanced equations for both steps.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Chemical Reactions and Equations', 3, 'A is Copper (Cu) and B is Copper(II) oxide (CuO). Step 1: 2Cu + O₂ -> 2CuO (black). Step 2: CuO + H₂ -> Cu + H₂O (reddish-brown restored).', 'Identification of A & B (1M) + Equation 1 (1M) + Equation 2 (1M)', NULL, 'A is Copper (Cu) and B is Copper(II) oxide (CuO). Step 1: 2Cu + O₂ -> 2CuO (black). Step 2: CuO + H₂ -> Cu + H₂O (reddish-brown restored).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000f', 'State three types of decomposition reactions with one balanced chemical equation for each.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Chemical Reactions and Equations', 3, '(1) Thermal: CaCO₃ -> CaO + CO₂. (2) Electrolytic: 2H₂O -> 2H₂ + O₂. (3) Photolytic: 2AgBr -> 2Ag + Br₂.', 'Thermal (1M) + Electrolytic (1M) + Photolytic (1M)', NULL, '(1) Thermal: CaCO₃ -> CaO + CO₂. (2) Electrolytic: 2H₂O -> 2H₂ + O₂. (3) Photolytic: 2AgBr -> 2Ag + Br₂.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000010', '(a) Define redox reaction. Identify the oxidised substance, reduced substance, oxidising agent, and reducing agent in: MnO₂ + 4HCl -> MnCl₂ + 2H₂O + Cl₂.
(b) What is rancidity? Mention two ways to prevent rancidity in food items.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Science', 'Chemical Reactions and Equations', 5, '(a) Redox involves simultaneous oxidation and reduction. HCl is oxidised to Cl₂; MnO₂ is reduced to MnCl₂. Oxidising agent: MnO₂; Reducing agent: HCl. (b) Rancidity is aerial oxidation of fats/oils producing bad smell/taste. Prevention: nitrogen flushing in packets, adding antioxidants (BHA/BHT), refrigeration/airtight containers.', 'Redox definition + 4 identifications (3M) + Rancidity & 2 methods (2M)', NULL, '(a) Redox involves simultaneous oxidation and reduction. HCl is oxidised to Cl₂; MnO₂ is reduced to MnCl₂. Oxidising agent: MnO₂; Reducing agent: HCl. (b) Rancidity is aerial oxidation of fats/oils producing bad smell/taste. Prevention: nitrogen flushing in packets, adding antioxidants (BHA/BHT), refrigeration/airtight containers.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000011', '(a) Balance: (i) Fe + H₂O -> Fe₃O₄ + H₂, (ii) BaCl₂ + Al₂(SO₄)₃ -> AlCl₃ + BaSO₄.
(b) Explain displacement vs double displacement reactions with one equation each.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Science', 'Chemical Reactions and Equations', 5, '(a)(i) 3Fe + 4H₂O -> Fe₃O₄ + 4H₂; (ii) 3BaCl₂ + Al₂(SO₄)₃ -> 2AlCl₃ + 3BaSO₄. (b) Displacement: more reactive element displaces less reactive one (Fe + CuSO₄ -> FeSO₄ + Cu). Double displacement: mutual exchange of ions (Na₂SO₄ + BaCl₂ -> BaSO₄ + 2NaCl).', 'Balancing 2 equations (2M) + Distinction & equations (3M)', NULL, '(a)(i) 3Fe + 4H₂O -> Fe₃O₄ + 4H₂; (ii) 3BaCl₂ + Al₂(SO₄)₃ -> 2AlCl₃ + 3BaSO₄. (b) Displacement: more reactive element displaces less reactive one (Fe + CuSO₄ -> FeSO₄ + Cu). Double displacement: mutual exchange of ions (Na₂SO₄ + BaCl₂ -> BaSO₄ + 2NaCl).', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000012', 'Based on the passage: (i) Write the chemical equation for slaking of quick lime. (ii) Is it exothermic or endothermic? (iii) Why do walls shine 2-3 days after whitewashing? (iv) Name the product formed on the wall.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Science', 'Chemical Reactions and Equations', 4, '(i) CaO(s) + H₂O(l) -> Ca(OH)₂(aq) + Heat. (ii) Exothermic reaction. (iii) Ca(OH)₂ reacts with atmospheric CO₂ to form a thin shiny layer of insoluble CaCO₃. (iv) Calcium carbonate (CaCO₃).', 'Equation (1M) + Nature (1M) + Explanation (1M) + Product name (1M)', 'A mason adds water to calcium oxide lumps in a bucket to prepare whitewash. The bucket turns very hot with a hissing sound. After applying the mixture onto walls, the surface appears dull initially, then turns bright white after two to three days.', '(i) CaO(s) + H₂O(l) -> Ca(OH)₂(aq) + Heat. (ii) Exothermic reaction. (iii) Ca(OH)₂ reacts with atmospheric CO₂ to form a thin shiny layer of insoluble CaCO₃. (iv) Calcium carbonate (CaCO₃).', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000013', 'From the electrolysis setup: (i) Identify gas collected at cathode and anode. (ii) Why is one gas double the volume of the other? (iii) Why are a few drops of dilute H₂SO₄ added to water? (iv) How do you test the gas collected in larger volume?', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Science', 'Chemical Reactions and Equations', 4, '(i) Cathode: H₂; Anode: O₂. (ii) Water formula H₂O has 2 atoms of H per 1 atom of O. (iii) Pure water is a poor conductor; dilute H₂SO₄ provides ions to increase conductivity. (iv) Bringing a burning splinter near it produces a pop sound.', '4 sub-parts × 1M each = 4M', 'A teacher sets up an apparatus for electrolysis of water using two graphite electrodes connected to a 6V battery and inverted test tubes filled with acidulated water. Gas bubbles start collecting at different rates in the two test tubes.', '(i) Cathode: H₂; Anode: O₂. (ii) Water formula H₂O has 2 atoms of H per 1 atom of O. (iii) Pure water is a poor conductor; dilute H₂SO₄ provides ions to increase conductivity. (iv) Bringing a burning splinter near it produces a pop sound.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000014', 'Which natural indicator turns reddish-brown in soap solution?', '{"A":"Litmus","B":"Turmeric","C":"Methyl orange","D":"Phenolphthalein"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Acids, Bases and Salts', 1, 'Turmeric turns reddish-brown in basic soap solution.', NULL, NULL, 'Turmeric turns reddish-brown in basic soap solution.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000015', 'The pH of gastric juice in a healthy human stomach is roughly:', '{"A":"7.4","B":"1.2","C":"9.5","D":"5.5"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Acids, Bases and Salts', 1, 'Gastric juice contains dilute HCl with pH ~1.2.', NULL, NULL, 'Gastric juice contains dilute HCl with pH ~1.2.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000016', 'Baking powder is a mixture of baking soda and a mild edible acid such as:', '{"A":"Sulphuric acid","B":"Tartaric acid","C":"Nitric acid","D":"Oxalic acid"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Acids, Bases and Salts', 1, 'Tartaric acid neutralises bitter Na₂CO₃ formed on heating baking soda.', NULL, NULL, 'Tartaric acid neutralises bitter Na₂CO₃ formed on heating baking soda.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000017', 'Chemical formula of Plaster of Paris is:', '{"A":"CaSO₄·2H₂O","B":"CaSO₄·½H₂O","C":"CaOCl₂","D":"Na₂CO₃·10H₂O"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Acids, Bases and Salts', 1, 'Calcium sulphate hemihydrate (CaSO₄·½H₂O) is Plaster of Paris.', NULL, NULL, 'Calcium sulphate hemihydrate (CaSO₄·½H₂O) is Plaster of Paris.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000018', 'Tooth decay begins when the pH of the mouth falls below:', '{"A":"7.0","B":"6.5","C":"5.5","D":"8.0"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Acids, Bases and Salts', 1, 'Below pH 5.5, tooth enamel (calcium hydroxyapatite) starts corroding.', NULL, NULL, 'Below pH 5.5, tooth enamel (calcium hydroxyapatite) starts corroding.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000019', 'Chlor-alkali process manufactures which three products from aqueous NaCl?', '{"A":"NaOH, Cl₂, H₂","B":"Na₂CO₃, O₂, H₂","C":"NaHCO₃, Cl₂, CO₂","D":"CaOCl₂, H₂, O₂"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Acids, Bases and Salts', 1, '2NaCl(aq) + 2H₂O(l) -> 2NaOH(aq) + Cl₂(g) + H₂(g).', NULL, NULL, '2NaCl(aq) + 2H₂O(l) -> 2NaOH(aq) + Cl₂(g) + H₂(g).', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001a', 'Dry HCl gas does not turn dry blue litmus paper red because:', '{"A":"Dry HCl is basic","B":"Absence of water prevents dissociation into H⁺/H₃O⁺ ions","C":"Litmus reacts only with bases","D":"HCl evaporates immediately"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Acids, Bases and Salts', 1, 'Acids show acidic properties only in aqueous medium when H⁺ ions form.', NULL, NULL, 'Acids show acidic properties only in aqueous medium when H⁺ ions form.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001b', 'Assertion (A): While diluting concentrated sulphuric acid, acid should always be added slowly to water with constant stirring.
Reason (R): Dilution of concentrated acid is highly exothermic and adding water to acid may cause splashing and burns.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Acids, Bases and Salts', 1, 'Adding acid to water spreads heat into larger water volume safely.', NULL, NULL, 'Adding acid to water spreads heat into larger water volume safely.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001c', 'Assertion (A): Aqueous solution of glucose conducts electricity.
Reason (R): Glucose dissolves in water without producing free ions.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Acids, Bases and Salts', 1, 'A is false (glucose does not conduct electricity); R is true.', NULL, NULL, 'A is false (glucose does not conduct electricity); R is true.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001d', 'Why does distilled water not conduct electricity, whereas rainwater does?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Acids, Bases and Salts', 2, 'Distilled water is pure covalent H₂O without dissolved ions. Rainwater dissolves atmospheric CO₂/SO₂ forming carbonic/acidic ions (H⁺, HCO₃⁻) which conduct current.', 'Absence of ions (1M) + Ions in rainwater (1M)', NULL, 'Distilled water is pure covalent H₂O without dissolved ions. Rainwater dissolves atmospheric CO₂/SO₂ forming carbonic/acidic ions (H⁺, HCO₃⁻) which conduct current.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001e', 'Write the chemical equation for preparing bleaching powder and state two uses.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Acids, Bases and Salts', 2, 'Ca(OH)₂(s) + Cl₂(g) -> CaOCl₂(s) + H₂O(l). Uses: bleaching cotton/linen in textile industry and disinfecting drinking water.', 'Equation (1M) + Two uses (1M)', NULL, 'Ca(OH)₂(s) + Cl₂(g) -> CaOCl₂(s) + H₂O(l). Uses: bleaching cotton/linen in textile industry and disinfecting drinking water.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001f', 'Give the chemical name, formula, and one use each of (i) Washing soda and (ii) Baking soda.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Acids, Bases and Salts', 3, '(i) Washing soda: Sodium carbonate decahydrate (Na₂CO₃·10H₂O), used to remove permanent hardness of water. (ii) Baking soda: Sodium hydrogencarbonate (NaHCO₃), used as antacid and in soda-acid fire extinguishers.', '1.5M per salt', NULL, '(i) Washing soda: Sodium carbonate decahydrate (Na₂CO₃·10H₂O), used to remove permanent hardness of water. (ii) Baking soda: Sodium hydrogencarbonate (NaHCO₃), used as antacid and in soda-acid fire extinguishers.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000020', 'A baker forgot to add baking powder while making a cake, making it hard and small. Which ingredient makes cakes soft and spongy? Write the chemical reaction that occurs on heating it.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Acids, Bases and Salts', 3, 'Baking powder (NaHCO₃ + tartaric acid) makes cakes soft. On heating: 2NaHCO₃ --Heat--> Na₂CO₃ + H₂O + CO₂(g). Escaping CO₂ gas creates pores making cake spongy.', 'Ingredient (1M) + Reaction & CO₂ role (2M)', NULL, 'Baking powder (NaHCO₃ + tartaric acid) makes cakes soft. On heating: 2NaHCO₃ --Heat--> Na₂CO₃ + H₂O + CO₂(g). Escaping CO₂ gas creates pores making cake spongy.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000021', '(a) What is water of crystallisation? Explain with the action of heat on blue copper sulphate crystals.
(b) What is pH scale? How do farmers treat acidic soil and how do we get relief from bee sting?', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Science', 'Acids, Bases and Salts', 5, '(a) Fixed number of water molecules present in one formula unit of a salt (CuSO₄·5H₂O). Heating drives off 5H₂O leaving white anhydrous CuSO₄; adding water restores blue colour. (b) Scale 0–14 measuring H⁺ concentration. Acidic soil treated with quick lime/slaked lime/chalk; bee sting (methanoic acid) treated with mild base like baking soda.', 'Part (a) 2.5M + Part (b) 2.5M', NULL, '(a) Fixed number of water molecules present in one formula unit of a salt (CuSO₄·5H₂O). Heating drives off 5H₂O leaving white anhydrous CuSO₄; adding water restores blue colour. (b) Scale 0–14 measuring H⁺ concentration. Acidic soil treated with quick lime/slaked lime/chalk; bee sting (methanoic acid) treated with mild base like baking soda.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000022', 'From the table: (i) Identify the strong acid and strong base. (ii) Arrange Solutions A(pH 2), B(pH 7), C(pH 13), D(pH 5) in increasing order of H⁺ ion concentration. (iii) Which solution produces brisk effervescence with Na₂CO₃? (iv) What colour does universal indicator show for Solution B?', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Science', 'Acids, Bases and Salts', 4, '(i) Strong acid: A (pH 2); Strong base: C (pH 13). (ii) C < B < D < A. (iii) Solution A (or D, acid releases CO₂). (iv) Green (neutral pH 7).', '4 sub-parts × 1M = 4M', 'Four laboratory solutions A, B, C, and D were tested with universal indicator paper and found to have pH values 2, 7, 13, and 5 respectively.', '(i) Strong acid: A (pH 2); Strong base: C (pH 13). (ii) C < B < D < A. (iii) Solution A (or D, acid releases CO₂). (iv) Green (neutral pH 7).', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000023', 'Which metal is liquid at room temperature and which non-metal is liquid at room temperature?', '{"A":"Gallium and Iodine","B":"Mercury and Bromine","C":"Sodium and Chlorine","D":"Caesium and Sulphur"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Metals and Non-metals', 1, 'Mercury (metal) and Bromine (non-metal) are liquids at room temperature.', NULL, NULL, 'Mercury (metal) and Bromine (non-metal) are liquids at room temperature.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000024', 'An amphoteric oxide reacts with both acids and bases to form salt and water. Identify one:', '{"A":"Na₂O","B":"Al₂O₃","C":"SO₂","D":"CaO"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Metals and Non-metals', 1, 'Al₂O₃ and ZnO are amphoteric oxides.', NULL, NULL, 'Al₂O₃ and ZnO are amphoteric oxides.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000025', 'Which metal cannot displace hydrogen from dilute hydrochloric acid?', '{"A":"Zinc","B":"Iron","C":"Copper","D":"Magnesium"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Metals and Non-metals', 1, 'Copper lies below hydrogen in the reactivity series.', NULL, NULL, 'Copper lies below hydrogen in the reactivity series.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000026', 'Cinnabar (HgS) is converted to mercury oxide by:', '{"A":"Calcination","B":"Roasting","C":"Electrolytic reduction","D":"Thermit process"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Metals and Non-metals', 1, 'Sulphide ores are heated strongly in excess air by roasting: 2HgS + 3O₂ -> 2HgO + 2SO₂.', NULL, NULL, 'Sulphide ores are heated strongly in excess air by roasting: 2HgS + 3O₂ -> 2HgO + 2SO₂.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000027', 'Solder is an alloy used for welding electrical wires because of its low melting point. Its constituents are:', '{"A":"Copper and Zinc","B":"Lead and Tin","C":"Copper and Tin","D":"Iron and Carbon"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Metals and Non-metals', 1, 'Solder is an alloy of Lead (Pb) and Tin (Sn).', NULL, NULL, 'Solder is an alloy of Lead (Pb) and Tin (Sn).', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000028', 'Ionic compounds conduct electricity in:', '{"A":"Solid state only","B":"Molten or aqueous state only","C":"Gaseous state only","D":"Never conduct electricity"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Metals and Non-metals', 1, 'Ions are rigid in solid state but mobile in molten/aqueous solution.', NULL, NULL, 'Ions are rigid in solid state but mobile in molten/aqueous solution.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000029', 'Galvanisation protects iron from rusting by coating it with a thin layer of:', '{"A":"Tin","B":"Zinc","C":"Nickel","D":"Silver"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Metals and Non-metals', 1, 'Zinc provides sacrificial protection even if the coating scratches.', NULL, NULL, 'Zinc provides sacrificial protection even if the coating scratches.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002a', 'Assertion (A): Sodium and potassium are stored immersed in kerosene oil.
Reason (R): Sodium and potassium react vigorously with cold water and atmospheric oxygen, catching fire.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Metals and Non-metals', 1, 'Kerosene prevents contact with moisture and air.', NULL, NULL, 'Kerosene prevents contact with moisture and air.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002b', 'Assertion (A): Gold and platinum occur in free native state in nature.
Reason (R): Gold and platinum lie at the very top of the reactivity series.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Metals and Non-metals', 1, 'A is true; R is false (they lie at the bottom of reactivity series).', NULL, NULL, 'A is true; R is false (they lie at the bottom of reactivity series).', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002c', 'Why do ionic compounds have high melting and boiling points?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Metals and Non-metals', 2, 'Ionic compounds consist of oppositely charged ions held together by strong electrostatic forces of attraction, requiring high thermal energy to break the crystal lattice.', 'Strong electrostatic forces (1M) + High energy needed (1M)', NULL, 'Ionic compounds consist of oppositely charged ions held together by strong electrostatic forces of attraction, requiring high thermal energy to break the crystal lattice.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002d', 'Show the formation of Magnesium Chloride (MgCl₂) by electron transfer.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Metals and Non-metals', 2, 'Mg (2,8,2) loses 2 electrons to become Mg²⁺ (2,8). Two Cl atoms (2,8,7) each gain 1 electron to form two Cl⁻ (2,8,8) ions, forming [Mg²⁺] [Cl⁻]₂.', 'Electron transfer representation (1M) + Correct ionic structure (1M)', NULL, 'Mg (2,8,2) loses 2 electrons to become Mg²⁺ (2,8). Two Cl atoms (2,8,7) each gain 1 electron to form two Cl⁻ (2,8,8) ions, forming [Mg²⁺] [Cl⁻]₂.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002e', 'Differentiate between Roasting and Calcination with one chemical equation each.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Metals and Non-metals', 3, 'Roasting: heating sulphide ore in excess air (2ZnS + 3O₂ -> 2ZnO + 2SO₂). Calcination: heating carbonate ore in limited air (ZnCO₃ -> ZnO + CO₂).', 'Definitions (1M) + 2 equations (2M)', NULL, 'Roasting: heating sulphide ore in excess air (2ZnS + 3O₂ -> 2ZnO + 2SO₂). Calcination: heating carbonate ore in limited air (ZnCO₃ -> ZnO + CO₂).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002f', 'What is the Thermit reaction? Write its equation and mention one industrial application.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Metals and Non-metals', 3, 'Reduction of iron(III) oxide by aluminium powder: Fe₂O₃(s) + 2Al(s) -> 2Fe(l) + Al₂O₃(s) + Heat. Highly exothermic; molten iron is used to join cracked railway tracks and machine parts.', 'Reaction & explanation (1.5M) + Equation (1M) + Use (0.5M)', NULL, 'Reduction of iron(III) oxide by aluminium powder: Fe₂O₃(s) + 2Al(s) -> 2Fe(l) + Al₂O₃(s) + Heat. Highly exothermic; molten iron is used to join cracked railway tracks and machine parts.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000030', 'Explain the electrolytic refining of impure copper with a labelled setup description (anode, cathode, electrolyte, anode mud) and write electrode reactions.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Science', 'Metals and Non-metals', 5, 'Anode: thick impure copper block; Cathode: thin pure copper strip; Electrolyte: acidified CuSO₄ solution. On passing current, impure Cu dissolves (Cu -> Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻) and pure Cu deposits at cathode (Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ -> Cu). Less reactive impurities (Ag, Au) settle below anode as anode mud.', 'Anode/Cathode/Electrolyte (2M) + Reactions (2M) + Anode mud (1M)', NULL, 'Anode: thick impure copper block; Cathode: thin pure copper strip; Electrolyte: acidified CuSO₄ solution. On passing current, impure Cu dissolves (Cu -> Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻) and pure Cu deposits at cathode (Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ -> Cu). Less reactive impurities (Ag, Au) settle below anode as anode mud.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000031', '(i) Arrange P, Q, R, S in decreasing order of reactivity. (ii) Which metal is most suitable for joining railway tracks if paired with Fe₂O₃? (iii) Can Q displace R from RSO₄? (iv) Name a metal extracted by electrolysis of its molten chloride.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Science', 'Metals and Non-metals', 4, '(i) R > Q > P > S (e.g., if R=Na/Ca, Q=Zn, P=Fe, S=Cu based on test conditions). (ii) Aluminium (from reactivity above Fe). (iii) No, less reactive Q cannot displace more reactive R. (iv) Sodium / Magnesium / Calcium.', '4 × 1M', 'In a lab displacement test, metal P reacts with steam only, Q reacts slowly with hot water and readily with dilute HCl, R catches fire in cold water, and S shows no reaction even with concentrated HCl.', '(i) R > Q > P > S (e.g., if R=Na/Ca, Q=Zn, P=Fe, S=Cu based on test conditions). (ii) Aluminium (from reactivity above Fe). (iii) No, less reactive Q cannot displace more reactive R. (iv) Sodium / Magnesium / Calcium.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000032', 'Number of covalent bonds in a molecule of ethane (C₂H₆) is:', '{"A":"6","B":"7","C":"8","D":"5"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Carbon and its Compounds', 1, '6 C–H bonds + 1 C–C bond = 7 covalent bonds.', NULL, NULL, '6 C–H bonds + 1 C–C bond = 7 covalent bonds.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000033', 'Which functional group is present in propanone (CH₃COCH₃)?', '{"A":"Aldehyde (-CHO)","B":"Ketone (>C=O)","C":"Carboxylic acid (-COOH)","D":"Alcohol (-OH)"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Carbon and its Compounds', 1, 'Suffix -one indicates ketone (>C=O).', NULL, NULL, 'Suffix -one indicates ketone (>C=O).', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000034', 'Successive members of a homologous series differ by a molecular mass of:', '{"A":"12 u","B":"14 u","C":"16 u","D":"18 u"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Carbon and its Compounds', 1, '-CH₂- unit has mass 12 + 2(1) = 14 u.', NULL, NULL, '-CH₂- unit has mass 12 + 2(1) = 14 u.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000035', 'Adding alkaline KMnO₄ to warm ethanol converts it into:', '{"A":"Ethene","B":"Ethanoic acid","C":"Ethane","D":"Ethyl ethanoate"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Carbon and its Compounds', 1, 'Alkaline KMnO₄ oxidises ethanol (CH₃CH₂OH) to ethanoic acid (CH₃COOH).', NULL, NULL, 'Alkaline KMnO₄ oxidises ethanol (CH₃CH₂OH) to ethanoic acid (CH₃COOH).', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000036', 'Vegetable oils containing unsaturated fatty acids are converted to saturated fats by:', '{"A":"Esterification","B":"Hydrogenation with Ni catalyst","C":"Saponification","D":"Combustion"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Carbon and its Compounds', 1, 'Addition of H₂ across C=C using Ni/Pd catalyst is hydrogenation.', NULL, NULL, 'Addition of H₂ across C=C using Ni/Pd catalyst is hydrogenation.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000037', 'Ethanol reacts with ethanoic acid in presence of conc. H₂SO₄ to form a sweet-smelling substance called:', '{"A":"Soap","B":"Ester (Ethyl ethanoate)","C":"Ether","D":"Alkene"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Carbon and its Compounds', 1, 'Esterification forms sweet-smelling ethyl ethanoate used in perfumes/flavourings.', NULL, NULL, 'Esterification forms sweet-smelling ethyl ethanoate used in perfumes/flavourings.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000038', 'Why do soaps fail to produce good lather in hard water?', '{"A":"Soaps react with Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ ions to form insoluble scum","B":"Hard water boils too fast","C":"Soaps dissolve only in acid","D":"Hard water has no oxygen"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Carbon and its Compounds', 1, 'Ca²⁺/Mg²⁺ precipitate long-chain fatty acid salts as sticky scum.', NULL, NULL, 'Ca²⁺/Mg²⁺ precipitate long-chain fatty acid salts as sticky scum.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000039', 'Assertion (A): Diamond is extremely hard and does not conduct electricity whereas graphite is soft and a good conductor.
Reason (R): In diamond each carbon forms 4 rigid tetrahedral bonds leaving no free electrons, whereas in graphite each carbon forms 3 planar bonds leaving 1 delocalised electron.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Carbon and its Compounds', 1, 'Structure difference explains both hardness and conductivity.', NULL, NULL, 'Structure difference explains both hardness and conductivity.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003a', 'Assertion (A): Saturated hydrocarbons generally burn with a clean blue flame.
Reason (R): Saturated hydrocarbons contain a very high percentage of carbon leading to incomplete combustion.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Carbon and its Compounds', 1, 'A is true; R is false (unsaturated hydrocarbons have higher C% giving yellow sooty flame).', NULL, NULL, 'A is true; R is false (unsaturated hydrocarbons have higher C% giving yellow sooty flame).', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003b', 'What is a homologous series? Write the first two members of the alkene series.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Carbon and its Compounds', 2, 'A series of organic compounds having the same functional group and similar chemical properties, differing by -CH₂-. First two alkenes: Ethene (C₂H₄) and Propene (C₃H₆).', 'Definition (1M) + First two alkenes (1M)', NULL, 'A series of organic compounds having the same functional group and similar chemical properties, differing by -CH₂-. First two alkenes: Ethene (C₂H₄) and Propene (C₃H₆).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003c', 'Draw the electron dot structure of ethyne (C₂H₂) and carbon dioxide (CO₂).', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Carbon and its Compounds', 2, 'C₂H₂ has H:C:::C:H (triple bond between C atoms). CO₂ has :O::C::O: (double bond on each side of C).', 'Ethyne dot structure (1M) + CO₂ dot structure (1M)', NULL, 'C₂H₂ has H:C:::C:H (triple bond between C atoms). CO₂ has :O::C::O: (double bond on each side of C).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003d', 'Give the IUPAC names and structural formulae of two isomers of butane (C₄H₁₀). Why can propane (C₃H₈) not show structural isomerism?', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Carbon and its Compounds', 3, 'Butane isomers: (1) Butane (straight chain CH₃-CH₂-CH₂-CH₃) and (2) 2-Methylpropane (branched CH₃-CH(CH₃)-CH₃). Propane has only 3 carbons, so branching at any carbon yields the same straight chain.', '2 isomers with names (2M) + Reason for propane (1M)', NULL, 'Butane isomers: (1) Butane (straight chain CH₃-CH₂-CH₂-CH₃) and (2) 2-Methylpropane (branched CH₃-CH(CH₃)-CH₃). Propane has only 3 carbons, so branching at any carbon yields the same straight chain.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003e', 'How are esters prepared? Write a balanced chemical equation and explain saponification as the reverse process.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Carbon and its Compounds', 3, 'Esterification: CH₃COOH + C₂H₅OH --(conc. H₂SO₄)--> CH₃COOC₂H₅ + H₂O. Saponification: alkaline hydrolysis of ester: CH₃COOC₂H₅ + NaOH -> CH₃COONa + C₂H₅OH (used in soap making).', 'Esterification (1.5M) + Saponification (1.5M)', NULL, 'Esterification: CH₃COOH + C₂H₅OH --(conc. H₂SO₄)--> CH₃COOC₂H₅ + H₂O. Saponification: alkaline hydrolysis of ester: CH₃COOC₂H₅ + NaOH -> CH₃COONa + C₂H₅OH (used in soap making).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003f', '(a) Explain the cleansing action of soap with the concept of micelle formation.
(b) What happens when ethanol is heated at 443 K with excess concentrated H₂SO₄? Write the equation and role of H₂SO₄.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Science', 'Carbon and its Compounds', 5, '(a) Soap molecules have a hydrophilic ionic head (-COO⁻Na⁺) and hydrophobic hydrocarbon tail. In water, tails point inward trapping oily dirt at the centre while ionic heads face outward forming spherical micelles that rinse away with water. (b) Dehydration forms ethene: CH₃CH₂OH --(conc. H₂SO₄, 443K)--> CH₂=CH₂ + H₂O. Conc. H₂SO₄ acts as dehydrating agent.', 'Micelle mechanism (3M) + Dehydration equation & role (2M)', NULL, '(a) Soap molecules have a hydrophilic ionic head (-COO⁻Na⁺) and hydrophobic hydrocarbon tail. In water, tails point inward trapping oily dirt at the centre while ionic heads face outward forming spherical micelles that rinse away with water. (b) Dehydration forms ethene: CH₃CH₂OH --(conc. H₂SO₄, 443K)--> CH₂=CH₂ + H₂O. Conc. H₂SO₄ acts as dehydrating agent.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000040', '(i) Identify X, Y, Z. (ii) Write the reaction of X with sodium metal. (iii) Why is glacial acetic acid named so? (iv) Distinguish between X and Y using sodium carbonate.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Science', 'Carbon and its Compounds', 4, '(i) X = Ethanol (C₂H₅OH), Y = Ethanoic acid (CH₃COOH), Z = Ethene (C₂H₄). (ii) 2C₂H₅OH + 2Na -> 2C₂H₅ONa + H₂. (iii) Pure ethanoic acid freezes at 290 K into glacier-like crystals in winter. (iv) Y gives brisk effervescence of CO₂ with Na₂CO₃, X does not.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'An organic compound X (C₂H₆O), an active ingredient of cough syrups, is oxidised by acidified potassium dichromate to form carboxylic acid Y. When X is heated with excess conc. H₂SO₄ at 443 K, an unsaturated gas Z is produced.', '(i) X = Ethanol (C₂H₅OH), Y = Ethanoic acid (CH₃COOH), Z = Ethene (C₂H₄). (ii) 2C₂H₅OH + 2Na -> 2C₂H₅ONa + H₂. (iii) Pure ethanoic acid freezes at 290 K into glacier-like crystals in winter. (iv) Y gives brisk effervescence of CO₂ with Na₂CO₃, X does not.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000041', 'Which enzyme present in human saliva initiates starch digestion?', '{"A":"Pepsin","B":"Salivary amylase","C":"Trypsin","D":"Lipase"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Life Processes', 1, 'Salivary amylase (ptyalin) breaks starch into maltose.', NULL, NULL, 'Salivary amylase (ptyalin) breaks starch into maltose.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000042', 'Breakdown of pyruvate to give CO₂, water, and energy takes place in:', '{"A":"Cytoplasm","B":"Mitochondria","C":"Chloroplast","D":"Nucleus"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Life Processes', 1, 'Aerobic Krebs cycle and oxidative phosphorylation occur in mitochondria.', NULL, NULL, 'Aerobic Krebs cycle and oxidative phosphorylation occur in mitochondria.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000043', 'Which blood vessel carries oxygenated blood from lungs to the left atrium of human heart?', '{"A":"Pulmonary artery","B":"Pulmonary vein","C":"Vena cava","D":"Aorta"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Life Processes', 1, 'Pulmonary vein carries oxygenated blood from lungs to left atrium.', NULL, NULL, 'Pulmonary vein carries oxygenated blood from lungs to left atrium.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000044', 'The basic filtration unit in human kidneys is the:', '{"A":"Neuron","B":"Nephron","C":"Alveolus","D":"Villus"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Life Processes', 1, 'Each kidney contains ~1 million nephrons for ultrafiltration.', NULL, NULL, 'Each kidney contains ~1 million nephrons for ultrafiltration.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000045', 'Translocation of sucrose in plants occurs through:', '{"A":"Xylem vessels","B":"Phloem sieve tubes (requiring ATP)","C":"Root hairs","D":"Guard cells"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Life Processes', 1, 'Phloem actively transports food using ATP osmotic pressure.', NULL, NULL, 'Phloem actively transports food using ATP osmotic pressure.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000046', 'Normal systolic and diastolic blood pressure of a healthy human adult is:', '{"A":"80/120 mmHg","B":"120/80 mmHg","C":"140/90 mmHg","D":"100/60 mmHg"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Life Processes', 1, '120 mmHg systolic / 80 mmHg diastolic.', NULL, NULL, '120 mmHg systolic / 80 mmHg diastolic.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000047', 'During strenuous exercise, muscle cramps occur due to accumulation of:', '{"A":"Ethanol","B":"Lactic acid","C":"Citric acid","D":"Uric acid"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Life Processes', 1, 'Lack of O₂ triggers anaerobic breakdown of pyruvate into lactic acid (3-carbon).', NULL, NULL, 'Lack of O₂ triggers anaerobic breakdown of pyruvate into lactic acid (3-carbon).', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000048', 'Assertion (A): Ventricles have thicker muscular walls than atria.
Reason (R): Ventricles have to pump blood into various body organs and lungs under higher pressure.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Life Processes', 1, 'Higher pumping distance requires thicker myocardium.', NULL, NULL, 'Higher pumping distance requires thicker myocardium.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000049', 'Assertion (A): Herbivores like cows have a longer small intestine than carnivores like tigers.
Reason (R): Cellulose digestion in plant food takes longer time than meat digestion.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Life Processes', 1, 'Longer small intestine allows complete cellulose breakdown.', NULL, NULL, 'Longer small intestine allows complete cellulose breakdown.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004a', 'What is the role of bile juice and hydrochloric acid in human digestion?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Life Processes', 2, 'HCl creates acidic medium (pH ~1.8) to activate pepsin and kills swallowed bacteria. Bile emulsifies large fat globules into small micelles and neutralises acidic chyme for pancreatic enzymes.', 'HCl role (1M) + Bile role (1M)', NULL, 'HCl creates acidic medium (pH ~1.8) to activate pepsin and kills swallowed bacteria. Bile emulsifies large fat globules into small micelles and neutralises acidic chyme for pancreatic enzymes.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004b', 'State two ways in which terrestrial organisms and aquatic organisms differ in breathing rate.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Life Processes', 2, 'Aquatic organisms breathe much faster because dissolved oxygen concentration in water is very low compared to oxygen percentage (~21%) in air.', 'O₂ availability contrast (1M) + Rate difference (1M)', NULL, 'Aquatic organisms breathe much faster because dissolved oxygen concentration in water is very low compared to oxygen percentage (~21%) in air.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004c', 'Describe the three events that occur during photosynthesis in green plants.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Life Processes', 3, '(1) Absorption of light energy by chlorophyll. (2) Conversion of light energy to chemical energy and splitting of water molecules into H₂ and O₂. (3) Reduction of carbon dioxide to carbohydrates (glucose).', '1M per event', NULL, '(1) Absorption of light energy by chlorophyll. (2) Conversion of light energy to chemical energy and splitting of water molecules into H₂ and O₂. (3) Reduction of carbon dioxide to carbohydrates (glucose).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004d', 'Explain double circulation in human beings. Why is it necessary?', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Life Processes', 3, 'Blood passes through the heart twice per complete body circuit: pulmonary circulation (Heart -> Lungs -> Heart) and systemic circulation (Heart -> Body -> Heart). Separating oxygenated and deoxygenated blood ensures high O₂ supply to support warm-blooded high energy demand.', 'Two circuits (1.5M) + Necessity (1.5M)', NULL, 'Blood passes through the heart twice per complete body circuit: pulmonary circulation (Heart -> Lungs -> Heart) and systemic circulation (Heart -> Body -> Heart). Separating oxygenated and deoxygenated blood ensures high O₂ supply to support warm-blooded high energy demand.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004e', 'Explain the structure of a nephron and trace the three main steps of urine formation (glomerular filtration, selective reabsorption, tubular secretion).', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Science', 'Life Processes', 5, 'Nephron has Bowman''s capsule enclosing glomerulus capillary tuft, PCT, Loop of Henle, DCT, collecting duct. (1) Glomerular filtration filters water, glucose, amino acids, urea into Bowman''s capsule. (2) Useful glucose, amino acids, salts, excess water are selectively reabsorbed into peritubular capillaries. (3) Additional wastes/ions are secreted before urine drains via ureter.', 'Structure (2M) + Three urine formation steps (3M)', NULL, 'Nephron has Bowman''s capsule enclosing glomerulus capillary tuft, PCT, Loop of Henle, DCT, collecting duct. (1) Glomerular filtration filters water, glucose, amino acids, urea into Bowman''s capsule. (2) Useful glucose, amino acids, salts, excess water are selectively reabsorbed into peritubular capillaries. (3) Additional wastes/ions are secreted before urine drains via ureter.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004f', '(i) Why are alveoli balloon-like and richly supplied with capillaries? (ii) Name the respiratory pigment in human RBCs. (iii) Why do lungs always retain a residual volume of air? (iv) How is carbon dioxide mainly transported in blood?', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Science', 'Life Processes', 4, '(i) Maximises surface area for rapid gas diffusion. (ii) Haemoglobin. (iii) Ensures sufficient time for O₂ absorption and CO₂ release between breaths. (iv) Mostly dissolved in plasma (and bicarbonate ions).', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Inside the human thorax, inhaled air moves through trachea, bronchi, and bronchioles into millions of thin-walled alveolar sacs surrounded by dense blood capillary networks.', '(i) Maximises surface area for rapid gas diffusion. (ii) Haemoglobin. (iii) Ensures sufficient time for O₂ absorption and CO₂ release between breaths. (iv) Mostly dissolved in plasma (and bicarbonate ions).', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000050', 'The gap between the axon terminal of one neuron and dendrite of the next neuron is called:', '{"A":"Axon hillock","B":"Synapse","C":"Myelin sheath","D":"Node of Ranvier"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Control and Coordination', 1, 'Synapse transmits impulses chemically via neurotransmitters.', NULL, NULL, 'Synapse transmits impulses chemically via neurotransmitters.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000051', 'Which part of the human brain maintains posture and equilibrium of the body?', '{"A":"Cerebrum","B":"Cerebellum","C":"Medulla","D":"Pons"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Control and Coordination', 1, 'Cerebellum coordinates voluntary balance and posture.', NULL, NULL, 'Cerebellum coordinates voluntary balance and posture.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000052', 'Which plant hormone promotes cell division and delays leaf senescence?', '{"A":"Auxin","B":"Gibberellin","C":"Cytokinin","D":"Abscisic acid"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Control and Coordination', 1, 'Cytokinins promote rapid cell division (high in fruits/seeds).', NULL, NULL, 'Cytokinins promote rapid cell division (high in fruits/seeds).', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000053', 'Deficiency of iodine in diet causes goitre due to insufficient secretion of:', '{"A":"Insulin","B":"Adrenaline","C":"Thyroxine","D":"Growth hormone"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Control and Coordination', 1, 'Thyroid needs iodine to synthesise thyroxine hormone.', NULL, NULL, 'Thyroid needs iodine to synthesise thyroxine hormone.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000054', 'Sudden drooping of Mimosa pudica (touch-me-not) leaves on touching is an example of:', '{"A":"Phototropism","B":"Nastic movement (thigmonasty)","C":"Geotropism","D":"Chemotropism"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Control and Coordination', 1, 'Non-directional rapid turgor loss in pulvini cells is nastic movement.', NULL, NULL, 'Non-directional rapid turgor loss in pulvini cells is nastic movement.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000055', 'Growth of a pollen tube towards the ovule is an example of:', '{"A":"Hydrotropism","B":"Chemotropism","C":"Geotropism","D":"Phototropism"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Control and Coordination', 1, 'Chemical stimulus guides pollen tube growth.', NULL, NULL, 'Chemical stimulus guides pollen tube growth.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000056', 'Emergency hormone secreted during fear or stress to prepare for fight-or-flight is:', '{"A":"Oestrogen","B":"Adrenaline","C":"Testosterone","D":"Thyroxine"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Control and Coordination', 1, 'Adrenaline from adrenal glands boosts heart rate and blood flow to muscles.', NULL, NULL, 'Adrenaline from adrenal glands boosts heart rate and blood flow to muscles.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000057', 'Assertion (A): Reflex arcs have evolved in animals for quick protective responses.
Reason (R): Thinking processes in the brain involve complex neuron networks and take longer time than spinal reflex loops.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Control and Coordination', 1, 'Spinal cord handles rapid reflexes directly while informing the brain.', NULL, NULL, 'Spinal cord handles rapid reflexes directly while informing the brain.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000058', 'Assertion (A): Abscisic acid (ABA) is called a growth-promoting hormone.
Reason (R): Abscisic acid causes wilting of leaves and dormancy.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Control and Coordination', 1, 'A is false (ABA inhibits growth); R is true.', NULL, NULL, 'A is false (ABA inhibits growth); R is true.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000059', 'How does auxin promote bending of a plant shoot towards sunlight?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Control and Coordination', 2, 'When light shines from one side, auxin diffuses toward the shaded side of the shoot tip, stimulating shaded cells to elongate faster and causing the stem to bend toward light.', 'Auxin migration to shaded side (1M) + Faster cell elongation (1M)', NULL, 'When light shines from one side, auxin diffuses toward the shaded side of the shoot tip, stimulating shaded cells to elongate faster and causing the stem to bend toward light.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005a', 'Name the hormone secreted by pancreas and state the disease caused by its deficiency.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Control and Coordination', 2, 'Insulin hormone (regulates blood glucose level). Deficiency causes Diabetes mellitus.', 'Hormone & function (1M) + Disease (1M)', NULL, 'Insulin hormone (regulates blood glucose level). Deficiency causes Diabetes mellitus.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005b', 'Trace the sequence of events in a reflex arc when you accidentally touch a hot pan.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Control and Coordination', 3, 'Receptor in skin detects heat -> Sensory neuron carries impulse to spinal cord -> Relay neuron (interneuron) processes signal -> Motor neuron carries impulse to effector (arm muscle) -> Muscle contracts and hand withdraws immediately.', 'Step-by-step reflex arc pathway (3M)', NULL, 'Receptor in skin detects heat -> Sensory neuron carries impulse to spinal cord -> Relay neuron (interneuron) processes signal -> Motor neuron carries impulse to effector (arm muscle) -> Muscle contracts and hand withdraws immediately.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005c', 'Compare nervous control and hormonal (chemical) control in animals on three parameters.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Control and Coordination', 3, '(1) Medium: electrical/chemical across neurons vs hormones via blood. (2) Speed: very rapid vs slow/sustained. (3) Reach: localised target vs widespread body cells.', '3 × 1M points', NULL, '(1) Medium: electrical/chemical across neurons vs hormones via blood. (2) Speed: very rapid vs slow/sustained. (3) Reach: localised target vs widespread body cells.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005d', 'Draw/describe the major regions of the human brain (Forebrain, Midbrain, Hindbrain) and list one specific function of Cerebrum, Hypothalamus, Cerebellum, and Medulla oblongata.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Science', 'Control and Coordination', 5, 'Forebrain (Cerebrum: thinking/memory/voluntary actions; Hypothalamus: hunger/thermoregulation), Midbrain (visual/auditory reflexes), Hindbrain (Pons: breathing relay; Cerebellum: balance/posture; Medulla: involuntary heart beat, BP, vomiting, salivation).', 'Three divisions (1M) + 4 specific functions (4M)', NULL, 'Forebrain (Cerebrum: thinking/memory/voluntary actions; Hypothalamus: hunger/thermoregulation), Midbrain (visual/auditory reflexes), Hindbrain (Pons: breathing relay; Cerebellum: balance/posture; Medulla: involuntary heart beat, BP, vomiting, salivation).', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005e', '(i) Name the endocrine gland and hormone involved. (ii) Which feedback mechanism regulates its release? (iii) Suggest dietary advice for this patient. (iv) Contrast this condition with dwarfism.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Science', 'Control and Coordination', 4, '(i) Pancreas; Insulin. (ii) Negative feedback: high blood glucose stimulates beta cells to secrete insulin; normal glucose stops release. (iii) Controlled balanced diet low in simple sugars + regular exercise. (iv) Dwarfism is due to low pituitary Growth Hormone, whereas diabetes involves pancreatic insulin.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'A middle-aged person experiences frequent urination, excessive thirst, and fatigue. Blood tests show elevated fasting glucose levels of 210 mg/dL, and doctors advise insulin regulation.', '(i) Pancreas; Insulin. (ii) Negative feedback: high blood glucose stimulates beta cells to secrete insulin; normal glucose stops release. (iii) Controlled balanced diet low in simple sugars + regular exercise. (iv) Dwarfism is due to low pituitary Growth Hormone, whereas diabetes involves pancreatic insulin.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005f', 'Amoeba reproduces asexually by binary fission in:', '{"A":"Any plane","B":"One specific longitudinal plane only","C":"Transverse plane only","D":"Multiple fission always"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'How do Organisms Reproduce?', 1, 'Amoeba divides in any plane; Leishmania divides in definite longitudinal plane.', NULL, NULL, 'Amoeba divides in any plane; Leishmania divides in definite longitudinal plane.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000060', 'Bryophyllum develops vegetative buds along the:', '{"A":"Stem nodes","B":"Root tubers","C":"Leaf margins (notches)","D":"Flower sepals"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'How do Organisms Reproduce?', 1, 'Fleshy notches of Bryophyllum leaves bear adventitious buds.', NULL, NULL, 'Fleshy notches of Bryophyllum leaves bear adventitious buds.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000061', 'In flowering plants, after fertilisation, the ovary and ovule develop respectively into:', '{"A":"Seed and Fruit","B":"Fruit and Seed","C":"Cotyledon and Plumule","D":"Endosperm and Seed coat"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'How do Organisms Reproduce?', 1, 'Ovary -> Fruit; Ovule -> Seed.', NULL, NULL, 'Ovary -> Fruit; Ovule -> Seed.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000062', 'Which surgical contraceptive method blocks sperm transport in human males?', '{"A":"Tubectomy","B":"Vasectomy","C":"Copper-T insertion","D":"Oral pills"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'How do Organisms Reproduce?', 1, 'Vasectomy cuts/ties the vas deferens; tubectomy blocks fallopian tubes.', NULL, NULL, 'Vasectomy cuts/ties the vas deferens; tubectomy blocks fallopian tubes.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000063', 'Site of fertilisation in the human female reproductive system is:', '{"A":"Uterus","B":"Vagina","C":"Oviduct (Fallopian tube)","D":"Ovary"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'How do Organisms Reproduce?', 1, 'Sperm fertilises ovum in the fallopian tube.', NULL, NULL, 'Sperm fertilises ovum in the fallopian tube.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000064', 'Rhizopus (bread mould) reproduces rapidly by:', '{"A":"Fragmentation","B":"Spore formation inside sporangia","C":"Budding","D":"Regeneration"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'How do Organisms Reproduce?', 1, 'Tiny spores with thick walls disperse and germinate on moist bread.', NULL, NULL, 'Tiny spores with thick walls disperse and germinate on moist bread.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000065', 'Which sexually transmitted disease is caused by a virus rather than bacteria?', '{"A":"Gonorrhoea","B":"Syphilis","C":"HIV-AIDS and Warts","D":"Cholera"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'How do Organisms Reproduce?', 1, 'HIV-AIDS and genital warts are viral STDs.', NULL, NULL, 'HIV-AIDS and genital warts are viral STDs.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000066', 'Assertion (A): DNA copying during reproduction is accompanied by slight variations.
Reason (R): Biochemical reactions of DNA replication are not 100% accurate and subtle changes lead to variation useful for evolution.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'How do Organisms Reproduce?', 1, 'Controlled inaccuracies provide raw variation for survival.', NULL, NULL, 'Controlled inaccuracies provide raw variation for survival.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000067', 'Assertion (A): Copper-T is an oral hormonal contraceptive pill.
Reason (R): Copper-T is an intrauterine device (IUD) placed inside the uterus to prevent implantation.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'How do Organisms Reproduce?', 1, 'A is false (Copper-T is an IUD, not a pill); R is true.', NULL, NULL, 'A is false (Copper-T is an IUD, not a pill); R is true.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000068', 'Why is vegetative propagation practised for growing banana, rose, and jasmine?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'How do Organisms Reproduce?', 2, 'It helps raise plants that have lost the capacity to produce viable seeds, allows faster flowering/fruiting, and preserves exact desirable parental traits.', '2 valid reasons × 1M', NULL, 'It helps raise plants that have lost the capacity to produce viable seeds, allows faster flowering/fruiting, and preserves exact desirable parental traits.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000069', 'List two functions performed by the human testes and state why they lie outside the abdominal cavity.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'How do Organisms Reproduce?', 2, 'Testes produce sperms and secrete testosterone. They lie in the scrotum because sperm formation requires a temperature 2–2.5°C lower than normal body temperature.', 'Functions (1M) + Scrotal temperature reason (1M)', NULL, 'Testes produce sperms and secrete testosterone. They lie in the scrotum because sperm formation requires a temperature 2–2.5°C lower than normal body temperature.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006a', 'Differentiate between self-pollination and cross-pollination. Name two agents of cross-pollination.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'How do Organisms Reproduce?', 3, 'Self: pollen transfers from anther to stigma of same flower or same plant. Cross: pollen transfers to stigma of another plant of same species (promotes variation). Agents: wind, water, insects, birds.', 'Comparison (2M) + Two agents (1M)', NULL, 'Self: pollen transfers from anther to stigma of same flower or same plant. Cross: pollen transfers to stigma of another plant of same species (promotes variation). Agents: wind, water, insects, birds.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006b', 'Explain the structure and function of the placenta during human pregnancy.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'How do Organisms Reproduce?', 3, 'Placenta is a disc-like tissue embedded in the uterine wall containing chorionic villi on embryo side and blood spaces on mother side. It supplies glucose and oxygen to foetus and removes CO₂ and excretory wastes.', 'Structure (1.5M) + Functions (1.5M)', NULL, 'Placenta is a disc-like tissue embedded in the uterine wall containing chorionic villi on embryo side and blood spaces on mother side. It supplies glucose and oxygen to foetus and removes CO₂ and excretory wastes.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006c', 'Describe the male and female reproductive parts of an angiosperm flower. Explain the events from pollination to seed formation (germination of pollen grain, double/syngamy fusion, post-fertilisation changes).', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Science', 'How do Organisms Reproduce?', 5, 'Stamen (anther + filament) is male; Pistil/Carpel (stigma + style + ovary with ovules) is female. Pollen lands on stigma -> pollen tube grows down style -> male germ cell fuses with egg in ovule forming zygote -> embryo inside seed; ovule develops tough coat becoming seed; ovary ripens into fruit.', 'Floral parts (2M) + Fertilisation & post-fertilisation changes (3M)', NULL, 'Stamen (anther + filament) is male; Pistil/Carpel (stigma + style + ovary with ovules) is female. Pollen lands on stigma -> pollen tube grows down style -> male germ cell fuses with egg in ovule forming zygote -> embryo inside seed; ovule develops tough coat becoming seed; ovary ripens into fruit.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006d', '(i) What happens to the uterine lining if the egg is not fertilised? (ii) What is this monthly event called? (iii) How many days does it generally last? (iv) Name one barrier method of contraception that also prevents STDs.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Science', 'How do Organisms Reproduce?', 4, '(i) Thick spongy blood-rich uterine lining breaks down and discharges as blood/mucus. (ii) Menstruation. (iii) About 2 to 8 days (28-day cycle). (iv) Condom.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Every month, one ovary releases a mature egg while the uterus prepares a thick, spongy lining rich in blood capillaries to receive a fertilised zygote.', '(i) Thick spongy blood-rich uterine lining breaks down and discharges as blood/mucus. (ii) Menstruation. (iii) About 2 to 8 days (28-day cycle). (iv) Condom.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006e', 'In Mendel''s monohybrid cross between pure tall (TT) and pure dwarf (tt) pea plants, the F₂ phenotypic ratio is:', '{"A":"1 : 1","B":"3 : 1","C":"9 : 3 : 3 : 1","D":"1 : 2 : 1"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Heredity', 1, 'F₂ shows 3 Tall (TT, Tt, Tt) : 1 Dwarf (tt).', NULL, NULL, 'F₂ shows 3 Tall (TT, Tt, Tt) : 1 Dwarf (tt).', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006f', 'In a dihybrid cross (RRYY × rryy), the F₂ phenotypic ratio obtained by Mendel is:', '{"A":"3 : 1","B":"1 : 2 : 1","C":"9 : 3 : 3 : 1","D":"2 : 1"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Heredity', 1, 'Independent assortment yields 9:3:3:1.', NULL, NULL, 'Independent assortment yields 9:3:3:1.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000070', 'In human beings, the sex of a newborn child is determined by:', '{"A":"Mother''s ovum chromosome","B":"Father''s sperm carrying X or Y chromosome","C":"Temperature of womb","D":"Mother''s nutrition"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Heredity', 1, 'Females are XX (produce only X); males are XY (produce X or Y sperms).', NULL, NULL, 'Females are XX (produce only X); males are XY (produce X or Y sperms).', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000071', 'How many pairs of autosomes are present in a normal human somatic cell?', '{"A":"23 pairs","B":"22 pairs","C":"1 pair","D":"46 pairs"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Heredity', 1, '22 pairs autosomes + 1 pair sex chromosomes = 23 pairs.', NULL, NULL, '22 pairs autosomes + 1 pair sex chromosomes = 23 pairs.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000072', 'Which term represents the genetic constitution of an organism such as Tt or TT?', '{"A":"Phenotype","B":"Genotype","C":"Allelomorph","D":"Mutation"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Heredity', 1, 'Genotype is internal genetic makeup; phenotype is observable appearance.', NULL, NULL, 'Genotype is internal genetic makeup; phenotype is observable appearance.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000073', 'Why did Mendel select garden pea (Pisum sativum) for his experiments?', '{"A":"Long life cycle","B":"Clear contrasting traits, short life cycle, easy self & cross pollination","C":"No visible seeds","D":"Polyploid nature"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Heredity', 1, 'Contrasting traits and short generation time made pea ideal.', NULL, NULL, 'Contrasting traits and short generation time made pea ideal.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000074', 'Assertion (A): The sex of a human baby is a matter of 50% chance and neither parent should be blamed for it.
Reason (R): Mother always contributes X chromosome; whether the child is XX (girl) or XY (boy) depends purely on which sperm (X or Y) fertilises the egg.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Heredity', 1, '50% X-sperm and 50% Y-sperm give equal probability.', NULL, NULL, '50% X-sperm and 50% Y-sperm give equal probability.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000075', 'Assertion (A): Acquired traits like a bodybuilder''s muscles are inherited by offspring.
Reason (R): Acquired changes occur only in somatic tissues and do not alter DNA in germ cells.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Heredity', 1, 'A is false (acquired traits are not inherited); R is true.', NULL, NULL, 'A is false (acquired traits are not inherited); R is true.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000076', 'State Mendel''s Law of Dominance with a suitable cross.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Heredity', 2, 'When two contrasting pure traits are crossed (TT × tt), only the dominant trait (Tall) expresses in F₁ generation (Tt), while the recessive trait (dwarf) remains suppressed.', 'Statement (1M) + Monohybrid example (1M)', NULL, 'When two contrasting pure traits are crossed (TT × tt), only the dominant trait (Tall) expresses in F₁ generation (Tt), while the recessive trait (dwarf) remains suppressed.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000077', 'How is sex determined in reptiles like turtles/crocodiles vs snails?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Heredity', 2, 'In some turtles and lizards, temperature at which fertilised eggs are incubated determines sex; in snails, individuals can change sex indicating non-genetic determination.', 'Environmental/temperature cue (1M) + Snails example (1M)', NULL, 'In some turtles and lizards, temperature at which fertilised eggs are incubated determines sex; in snails, individuals can change sex indicating non-genetic determination.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000078', 'Explain how the mechanism of DNA dictates the synthesis of proteins and controls a plant''s height.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Heredity', 3, 'Cellular DNA section (gene) provides information for synthesising a specific protein/enzyme. An efficient enzyme produces ample growth hormone (gibberellin/auxin) making the plant tall; an altered gene yields less efficient enzyme -> less hormone -> short plant.', 'Gene -> Protein -> Enzyme efficiency -> Hormone -> Trait (3M)', NULL, 'Cellular DNA section (gene) provides information for synthesising a specific protein/enzyme. An efficient enzyme produces ample growth hormone (gibberellin/auxin) making the plant tall; an altered gene yields less efficient enzyme -> less hormone -> short plant.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000079', 'A blue-eyed trait (bb) is recessive to brown eyes (BB). If two heterozygous brown-eyed parents (Bb × Bb) have children, draw a Punnett square to find the percentage chance of a blue-eyed child and the genotypic ratio.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Heredity', 3, 'Gametes: B, b × B, b -> BB (1), Bb (2), bb (1). Chance of blue eyes (bb) = 1/4 = 25%. Genotypic ratio = 1 BB : 2 Bb : 1 bb.', 'Cross/Punnett (1.5M) + 25% & ratio (1.5M)', NULL, 'Gametes: B, b × B, b -> BB (1), Bb (2), bb (1). Chance of blue eyes (bb) = 1/4 = 25%. Genotypic ratio = 1 BB : 2 Bb : 1 bb.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007a', 'Illustrate Mendel''s Dihybrid Cross between Round Yellow (RRYY) and Wrinkled Green (rryy) pea seeds up to F₂ generation. Write F₁ phenotype, F₂ phenotypic ratio, and the conclusion (Law of Independent Assortment).', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Science', 'Heredity', 5, 'P: RRYY × rryy -> F₁: RrYy (100% Round Yellow). Selfing RrYy × RrYy produces 4 gamete types (RY, Ry, rY, ry) -> 16 combinations: 9 Round Yellow : 3 Round Green : 3 Wrinkled Yellow : 1 Wrinkled Green. Proves traits inherit independently of one another.', 'Cross steps (2M) + Ratio 9:3:3:1 (1.5M) + Law explanation (1.5M)', NULL, 'P: RRYY × rryy -> F₁: RrYy (100% Round Yellow). Selfing RrYy × RrYy produces 4 gamete types (RY, Ry, rY, ry) -> 16 combinations: 9 Round Yellow : 3 Round Green : 3 Wrinkled Yellow : 1 Wrinkled Green. Proves traits inherit independently of one another.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007b', '(i) Write the F₁ genotype and phenotype. (ii) How many tall violet vs short white plants appear out of 160 F₂ progeny? (iii) Identify two parental combinations and two recombinant phenotypes in F₂. (iv) Which Mendel law is demonstrated?', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Science', 'Heredity', 4, '(i) TtVv, All Tall Violet. (ii) Tall Violet = (9/16)×160 = 90; Short White = (1/16)×160 = 10. (iii) Parental: Tall Violet, Short White; Recombinants: Tall White, Short Violet. (iv) Law of Independent Assortment.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'In a breeding experiment, pure tall plants bearing violet flowers (TTVV) are crossed with pure short plants bearing white flowers (ttvv), and the F₁ hybrids are self-pollinated to yield 160 F₂ plants.', '(i) TtVv, All Tall Violet. (ii) Tall Violet = (9/16)×160 = 90; Short White = (1/16)×160 = 10. (iii) Parental: Tall Violet, Short White; Recombinants: Tall White, Short Violet. (iv) Law of Independent Assortment.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007c', 'An object placed between focus F and pole P of a concave mirror forms an image that is:', '{"A":"Real, inverted, diminished","B":"Virtual, erect, magnified","C":"Real, inverted, same size","D":"Virtual, diminished"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Light – Reflection and Refraction', 1, 'Concave mirror acts as magnifying shaving/dentist mirror for u < f.', NULL, NULL, 'Concave mirror acts as magnifying shaving/dentist mirror for u < f.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007d', 'A convex lens of focal length +25 cm has optical power equal to:', '{"A":"+2.5 D","B":"+4.0 D","C":"-4.0 D","D":"+0.04 D"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Light – Reflection and Refraction', 1, 'P = 1/f(m) = 1/0.25 = +4.0 D.', NULL, NULL, 'P = 1/f(m) = 1/0.25 = +4.0 D.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007e', 'If refractive index of glass is 1.5 and speed of light in vacuum is 3×10⁸ m/s, speed of light in glass is:', '{"A":"2×10⁸ m/s","B":"4.5×10⁸ m/s","C":"1.5×10⁸ m/s","D":"2.5×10⁸ m/s"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Light – Reflection and Refraction', 1, 'v = c/n = 3×10⁸ / 1.5 = 2×10⁸ m/s.', NULL, NULL, 'v = c/n = 3×10⁸ / 1.5 = 2×10⁸ m/s.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007f', 'Which mirror always forms a diminished, virtual, and erect image for any finite object position?', '{"A":"Concave mirror","B":"Plane mirror","C":"Convex mirror","D":"Parabolic mirror"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Light – Reflection and Refraction', 1, 'Convex mirror is used as rear-view mirror for wide field of view.', NULL, NULL, 'Convex mirror is used as rear-view mirror for wide field of view.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000080', 'Linear magnification m = +1/3 for a spherical mirror indicates:', '{"A":"Concave mirror","B":"Convex mirror","C":"Plane mirror","D":"Real image"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Light – Reflection and Refraction', 1, '+ sign -> virtual/erect; |m| < 1 -> diminished -> Convex mirror.', NULL, NULL, '+ sign -> virtual/erect; |m| < 1 -> diminished -> Convex mirror.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000081', 'Absolute refractive index of any medium other than vacuum is always:', '{"A":"Less than 1","B":"Greater than 1","C":"Zero","D":"Negative"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Light – Reflection and Refraction', 1, 'Light travels fastest in vacuum (c > v), so n = c/v > 1.', NULL, NULL, 'Light travels fastest in vacuum (c > v), so n = c/v > 1.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000082', 'Assertion (A): Convex mirrors are preferred over plane mirrors as rear-view wing mirrors in vehicles.
Reason (R): Convex mirrors always produce an erect, diminished image and offer a much wider field of view.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Light – Reflection and Refraction', 1, 'Outward curvature widens driver visibility safely.', NULL, NULL, 'Outward curvature widens driver visibility safely.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000083', 'Assertion (A): Refractive index has unit metre per second (m/s).
Reason (R): Refractive index is the ratio of speeds of light in two media.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Light – Reflection and Refraction', 1, 'A is false (n is dimensionless); R is true.', NULL, NULL, 'A is false (n is dimensionless); R is true.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000084', 'State the two laws of refraction of light (including Snell''s law).', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Light – Reflection and Refraction', 2, '(1) Incident ray, refracted ray, and normal at point of incidence lie in the same plane. (2) Ratio sin i / sin r = constant (n₂₁) for a given pair of media and colour of light.', '1M per law', NULL, '(1) Incident ray, refracted ray, and normal at point of incidence lie in the same plane. (2) Ratio sin i / sin r = constant (n₂₁) for a given pair of media and colour of light.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000085', 'A ray of light enters obliquely from air into water. Does it bend towards or away from the normal and why?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Light – Reflection and Refraction', 2, 'It bends towards the normal because water is optically denser than air, so light slows down on entering water.', 'Direction (1M) + Reason (1M)', NULL, 'It bends towards the normal because water is optically denser than air, so light slows down on entering water.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000086', 'An object 4 cm tall is placed at 25 cm in front of a concave mirror of focal length 15 cm. Find the position, nature, and size of the image.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Light – Reflection and Refraction', 3, 'u = -25 cm, f = -15 cm. 1/v = 1/f - 1/u = -1/15 + 1/25 = -2/75 -> v = -37.5 cm (in front of mirror). m = -v/u = -(-37.5)/(-25) = -1.5. h'' = m×h = -6 cm (real, inverted, enlarged).', 'Formula & substitution (1M) + v = -37.5 cm (1M) + size & nature (1M)', NULL, 'u = -25 cm, f = -15 cm. 1/v = 1/f - 1/u = -1/15 + 1/25 = -2/75 -> v = -37.5 cm (in front of mirror). m = -v/u = -(-37.5)/(-25) = -1.5. h'' = m×h = -6 cm (real, inverted, enlarged).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000087', 'A convex lens forms a real and inverted image of a needle at a distance of 50 cm from it. Where is the needle placed if the image size equals the object size? Find the power of the lens.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Light – Reflection and Refraction', 3, 'Same-size real image forms at 2F -> v = +50 cm, u = -50 cm, so 2f = 50 cm -> f = +25 cm = +0.25 m. Power P = 1/0.25 = +4 D.', 'u = -50 cm (1.5M) + P = +4 D (1.5M)', NULL, 'Same-size real image forms at 2F -> v = +50 cm, u = -50 cm, so 2f = 50 cm -> f = +25 cm = +0.25 m. Power P = 1/0.25 = +4 D.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000088', 'Draw ray diagrams for image formation by a convex lens when object is at: (i) 2F₁, (ii) between F₁ and 2F₁, (iii) between optical centre O and F₁. State image position, size, and nature for each. Also write the lens formula and magnification formula.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Science', 'Light – Reflection and Refraction', 5, '(i) At 2F₂, same size, real & inverted. (ii) Beyond 2F₂, magnified, real & inverted. (iii) Same side as object, magnified, virtual & erect. Lens formula: 1/v - 1/u = 1/f; m = v/u = h''/h.', '3 cases with diagrams & properties (4M) + Formulae (1M)', NULL, '(i) At 2F₂, same size, real & inverted. (ii) Beyond 2F₂, magnified, real & inverted. (iii) Same side as object, magnified, virtual & erect. Lens formula: 1/v - 1/u = 1/f; m = v/u = h''/h.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000089', '(i) Identify the type of lens used. (ii) Where is the object placed to get focal point convergence? (iii) If beam converges at 20 cm from lens, calculate power of lens. (iv) Name one optical instrument using two convex lenses.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Science', 'Light – Reflection and Refraction', 4, '(i) Convex (converging) lens. (ii) At infinity (parallel Sun rays). (iii) f = +20 cm = +0.20 m -> P = +5 D. (iv) Compound microscope / Astronomical telescope.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'A student holds a lens toward distant sunlight and adjusts a paper screen until a bright sharp point spot forms 20 cm behind the lens, eventually charring the paper.', '(i) Convex (converging) lens. (ii) At infinity (parallel Sun rays). (iii) f = +20 cm = +0.20 m -> P = +5 D. (iv) Compound microscope / Astronomical telescope.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008a', 'Which part of the human eye controls the size of the pupil to regulate light entry?', '{"A":"Cornea","B":"Iris","C":"Retina","D":"Crystalline lens"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'The Human Eye and the Colourful World', 1, 'Iris muscles expand or contract pupil size.', NULL, NULL, 'Iris muscles expand or contract pupil size.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008b', 'Far point of a normal healthy human eye is:', '{"A":"25 cm","B":"2.5 m","C":"Infinity","D":"100 m"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'The Human Eye and the Colourful World', 1, 'Least distance of distinct vision is 25 cm; far point is infinity.', NULL, NULL, 'Least distance of distinct vision is 25 cm; far point is infinity.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008c', 'Myopia (short-sightedness) is corrected using a suitable:', '{"A":"Convex lens","B":"Concave lens","C":"Bifocal lens","D":"Cylindrical lens"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'The Human Eye and the Colourful World', 1, 'Concave (diverging) lens brings distant image back onto the retina.', NULL, NULL, 'Concave (diverging) lens brings distant image back onto the retina.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008d', 'The clear sky appears blue primarily due to:', '{"A":"Refraction of sunlight","B":"Rayleigh scattering of shorter blue wavelengths by fine atmospheric molecules","C":"Dispersion in clouds","D":"Total internal reflection"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'The Human Eye and the Colourful World', 1, 'Fine air molecules scatter shorter wavelengths (blue) much more strongly than red.', NULL, NULL, 'Fine air molecules scatter shorter wavelengths (blue) much more strongly than red.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008e', 'Which phenomenon explains twinkling of stars on a clear night?', '{"A":"Dispersion","B":"Atmospheric refraction through varying air density layers","C":"Tyndall effect","D":"Interference"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'The Human Eye and the Colourful World', 1, 'Refractive index fluctuations in moving air cause apparent star brightness to flicker.', NULL, NULL, 'Refractive index fluctuations in moving air cause apparent star brightness to flicker.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008f', 'Danger signal lights are painted red because red light:', '{"A":"Has shortest wavelength","B":"Has longest wavelength and is scattered the least by fog/smoke","C":"Travels fastest in vacuum","D":"Is absorbed by glass"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'The Human Eye and the Colourful World', 1, 'Red scatters minimum and remains visible from afar.', NULL, NULL, 'Red scatters minimum and remains visible from afar.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000090', 'Assertion (A): Planets do not twinkle like stars even though both are seen through the same atmosphere.
Reason (R): Planets are much closer to Earth and act as extended collections of many point sources whose individual twinkling variations average out to zero.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'The Human Eye and the Colourful World', 1, 'Extended source size averages atmospheric fluctuations.', NULL, NULL, 'Extended source size averages atmospheric fluctuations.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000091', 'Assertion (A): In hypermetropia, the image of a nearby object focuses in front of the retina.
Reason (R): In hypermetropia (far-sightedness), image of nearby objects forms behind the retina.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'The Human Eye and the Colourful World', 1, 'A is false; R is true.', NULL, NULL, 'A is false; R is true.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000092', 'What is power of accommodation of the human eye? Which muscles help change focal length of the eye lens?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'The Human Eye and the Colourful World', 2, 'Ability of the eye lens to adjust its focal length to focus near and distant objects clearly on the retina. Ciliary muscles relax/contract to alter lens curvature.', 'Definition (1M) + Ciliary muscles (1M)', NULL, 'Ability of the eye lens to adjust its focal length to focus near and distant objects clearly on the retina. Ciliary muscles relax/contract to alter lens curvature.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000093', 'Why does the Sun appear reddish at sunrise and sunset?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'The Human Eye and the Colourful World', 2, 'Near horizon, sunlight traverses a thicker layer of atmosphere; shorter blue wavelengths scatter away, leaving longer red wavelengths to reach our eyes.', 'Longer atmospheric path (1M) + Least scattering of red (1M)', NULL, 'Near horizon, sunlight traverses a thicker layer of atmosphere; shorter blue wavelengths scatter away, leaving longer red wavelengths to reach our eyes.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000094', 'What is Presbyopia? State its two causes and name the lens used to correct a person suffering from both myopia and hypermetropia.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'The Human Eye and the Colourful World', 3, 'Age-related defect losing accommodation for near vision due to weakening ciliary muscles and diminishing eye-lens flexibility. Corrected by bifocal lens (upper concave for distance, lower convex for reading).', 'Definition & causes (2M) + Bifocal lens (1M)', NULL, 'Age-related defect losing accommodation for near vision due to weakening ciliary muscles and diminishing eye-lens flexibility. Corrected by bifocal lens (upper concave for distance, lower convex for reading).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000095', 'A myopic person cannot see objects distinctly beyond 1.2 m. Calculate the nature and power of the corrective lens required to restore proper vision.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'The Human Eye and the Colourful World', 3, 'u = -∞, v = -1.2 m. 1/f = 1/v - 1/u = -1/1.2 -> f = -1.2 m (Concave lens). P = 1/(-1.2) = -0.83 D.', 'v & u values (1M) + Focal length & nature (1M) + Power -0.83 D (1M)', NULL, 'u = -∞, v = -1.2 m. 1/f = 1/v - 1/u = -1/1.2 -> f = -1.2 m (Concave lens). P = 1/(-1.2) = -0.83 D.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000096', 'Draw/explain dispersion of white light through a triangular glass prism. Why do different colours bend through different angles? How did Newton recombine the seven colours using an identical inverted second prism?', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Science', 'The Human Eye and the Colourful World', 5, 'White light splits into VIBGYOR spectrum because glass slows different wavelengths differently (violet shortest wavelength deviates most, red longest deviates least). Placing a second identical prism inverted adjacent to the first reverses the dispersion, recombining all seven colours back into an emergent white beam.', 'Dispersion & cause (3M) + Newton recombination experiment (2M)', NULL, 'White light splits into VIBGYOR spectrum because glass slows different wavelengths differently (violet shortest wavelength deviates most, red longest deviates least). Placing a second identical prism inverted adjacent to the first reverses the dispersion, recombining all seven colours back into an emergent white beam.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000097', '(i) What causes rainbow formation after rain? (ii) Name the three optical phenomena involved in rainbow formation inside a water droplet. (iii) In which direction relative to the Sun is a rainbow always observed? (iv) Why is astronauts'' sky black?', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Science', 'The Human Eye and the Colourful World', 4, '(i) Dispersion of sunlight by tiny atmospheric water droplets. (ii) Refraction, dispersion, and internal reflection. (iii) Always in the direction opposite to the Sun. (iv) Absence of atmosphere means no scattering.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Soon after an afternoon rain shower, a bright rainbow arch appears across the eastern sky while the Sun shines low in the west.', '(i) Dispersion of sunlight by tiny atmospheric water droplets. (ii) Refraction, dispersion, and internal reflection. (iii) Always in the direction opposite to the Sun. (iv) Absence of atmosphere means no scattering.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000098', 'SI unit of electric charge and electric power are respectively:', '{"A":"Ampere and Volt","B":"Coulomb and Watt","C":"Ohm and Joule","D":"Coulomb and Volt"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Electricity', 1, 'Charge: Coulomb (C); Power: Watt (W).', NULL, NULL, 'Charge: Coulomb (C); Power: Watt (W).', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000099', 'If a wire of resistance R is stretched to double its length (volume constant), its new resistance becomes:', '{"A":"2R","B":"4R","C":"R/2","D":"R/4"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Electricity', 1, 'L -> 2L and A -> A/2, so R'' = ρ(2L)/(A/2) = 4R.', NULL, NULL, 'L -> 2L and A -> A/2, so R'' = ρ(2L)/(A/2) = 4R.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000009a', 'Three resistors of 6 Ω each connected in parallel give an equivalent resistance of:', '{"A":"18 Ω","B":"2 Ω","C":"3 Ω","D":"0.5 Ω"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Electricity', 1, '1/Rp = 1/6 + 1/6 + 1/6 = 3/6 -> Rp = 2 Ω.', NULL, NULL, '1/Rp = 1/6 + 1/6 + 1/6 = 3/6 -> Rp = 2 Ω.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000009b', '1 kilowatt-hour (1 kWh commercial unit of electrical energy) equals:', '{"A":"3.6 × 10⁵ J","B":"3.6 × 10⁶ J","C":"1000 J","D":"3.6 × 10³ J"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Electricity', 1, '1000 W × 3600 s = 3.6 × 10⁶ J.', NULL, NULL, '1000 W × 3600 s = 3.6 × 10⁶ J.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000009c', 'Which alloy is commonly used for heating elements of electric irons and toasters?', '{"A":"Copper","B":"Nichrome","C":"Tungsten","D":"Manganin"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Electricity', 1, 'Nichrome has high resistivity and does not oxidise easily at red-hot temperatures.', NULL, NULL, 'Nichrome has high resistivity and does not oxidise easily at red-hot temperatures.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000009d', 'An electric bulb is rated 220 V and 100 W. Its resistance when glowing is:', '{"A":"484 Ω","B":"220 Ω","C":"100 Ω","D":"48.4 Ω"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Electricity', 1, 'R = V²/P = 220² / 100 = 48400 / 100 = 484 Ω.', NULL, NULL, 'R = V²/P = 220² / 100 = 48400 / 100 = 484 Ω.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000009e', 'Assertion (A): Tungsten is used exclusively for making filaments of incandescent electric bulbs.
Reason (R): Tungsten has a very high melting point (~3380°C) and retains strength at high temperature.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Electricity', 1, 'High melting point prevents filament melting while glowing.', NULL, NULL, 'High melting point prevents filament melting while glowing.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000009f', 'Assertion (A): In household circuits, appliances are connected in series.
Reason (R): In parallel circuits, every appliance receives full 220 V and can operate independently via separate switches.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Electricity', 1, 'A is false (household circuits use parallel connection); R is true.', NULL, NULL, 'A is false (household circuits use parallel connection); R is true.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a0', 'State Ohm''s law and draw the V–I graph nature for an ohmic metallic conductor at constant temperature.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Electricity', 2, 'At constant temperature, current I flowing through a metallic conductor is directly proportional to potential difference V across its ends (V = IR). The V–I graph is a straight line passing through the origin.', 'Statement (1M) + Straight-line graph through origin (1M)', NULL, 'At constant temperature, current I flowing through a metallic conductor is directly proportional to potential difference V across its ends (V = IR). The V–I graph is a straight line passing through the origin.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a1', 'List the four factors on which resistance of a cylindrical conductor depends.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Electricity', 2, '(1) Directly proportional to length L. (2) Inversely proportional to cross-sectional area A. (3) Nature of material (resistivity ρ). (4) Temperature.', '0.5M each × 4 = 2M', NULL, '(1) Directly proportional to length L. (2) Inversely proportional to cross-sectional area A. (3) Nature of material (resistivity ρ). (4) Temperature.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a2', 'Calculate the heat produced when 96,000 C of charge is transferred in 1 hour through a potential difference of 50 V.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Electricity', 3, 'H = V × Q (since I×t = Q and H = VIt) = 50 V × 96,000 C = 4,800,000 J = 4.8 × 10⁶ J = 4800 kJ.', 'Formula (1M) + Substitution (1M) + Correct answer 4.8×10⁶ J (1M)', NULL, 'H = V × Q (since I×t = Q and H = VIt) = 50 V × 96,000 C = 4,800,000 J = 4.8 × 10⁶ J = 4800 kJ.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a3', 'Show how you would connect three resistors each of resistance 6 Ω so that the combination has resistance of (i) 9 Ω and (ii) 4 Ω.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Electricity', 3, '(i) Connect two 6 Ω in parallel (Rp = 3 Ω) in series with the third 6 Ω -> 3 + 6 = 9 Ω. (ii) Connect two 6 Ω in series (Rs = 12 Ω) in parallel with the third 6 Ω -> (12×6)/(12+6) = 72/18 = 4 Ω.', '1.5M per combination', NULL, '(i) Connect two 6 Ω in parallel (Rp = 3 Ω) in series with the third 6 Ω -> 3 + 6 = 9 Ω. (ii) Connect two 6 Ω in series (Rs = 12 Ω) in parallel with the third 6 Ω -> (12×6)/(12+6) = 72/18 = 4 Ω.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a4', 'Derive the expression for equivalent resistance of three resistors R₁, R₂, R₃ connected in (a) Series and (b) Parallel. State one advantage of parallel wiring over series wiring.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Science', 'Electricity', 5, '(a) In series, I is same and V = V₁+V₂+V₃ -> IRs = IR₁+IR₂+IR₃ -> Rs = R₁+R₂+R₃. (b) In parallel, V is same and I = I₁+I₂+I₃ -> V/Rp = V/R₁+V/R₂+V/R₃ -> 1/Rp = 1/R₁+1/R₂+1/R₃. Advantage: failure of one device does not break the whole circuit.', 'Series derivation (2M) + Parallel derivation (2M) + Advantage (1M)', NULL, '(a) In series, I is same and V = V₁+V₂+V₃ -> IRs = IR₁+IR₂+IR₃ -> Rs = R₁+R₂+R₃. (b) In parallel, V is same and I = I₁+I₂+I₃ -> V/Rp = V/R₁+V/R₂+V/R₃ -> 1/Rp = 1/R₁+1/R₂+1/R₃. Advantage: failure of one device does not break the whole circuit.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a5', '(i) Calculate the total resistance of a 10 Ω heater and a 2 Ω resistor in series across 6 V. (ii) Find the current drawn from the battery. (iii) What potential difference appears across the 10 Ω heater? (iv) Calculate power consumed by the heater.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Science', 'Electricity', 4, '(i) Rs = 10 + 2 = 12 Ω. (ii) I = V/Rs = 6/12 = 0.5 A. (iii) V_heater = I×10 = 5 V. (iv) P = I²R = 0.25×10 = 2.5 W.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'A student wires a 10 Ω electric heater coil in series with a 2 Ω protective resistor across a 6 V DC battery and measures the current with an ammeter.', '(i) Rs = 10 + 2 = 12 Ω. (ii) I = V/Rs = 6/12 = 0.5 A. (iii) V_heater = I×10 = 5 V. (iv) P = I²R = 0.25×10 = 2.5 W.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a6', 'Magnetic field lines inside a long straight current-carrying solenoid are:', '{"A":"Concentric circles decreasing to zero","B":"Parallel equidistant straight lines indicating uniform field","C":"Perpendicular to axis","D":"Radially outward"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Magnetic Effects of Electric Current', 1, 'Field inside a solenoid is strong and uniform.', NULL, NULL, 'Field inside a solenoid is strong and uniform.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a7', 'Which rule determines the direction of force on a current-carrying conductor in a magnetic field?', '{"A":"Right-Hand Thumb Rule","B":"Fleming''s Left-Hand Rule","C":"Fleming''s Right-Hand Rule","D":"Snell''s Law"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Magnetic Effects of Electric Current', 1, 'Fleming''s Left-Hand Rule gives force direction (motor principle).', NULL, NULL, 'Fleming''s Left-Hand Rule gives force direction (motor principle).', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a8', 'In Indian domestic AC supply, frequency and potential difference between live and neutral wires are:', '{"A":"50 Hz and 220 V","B":"60 Hz and 110 V","C":"50 Hz and 110 V","D":"100 Hz and 220 V"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Magnetic Effects of Electric Current', 1, 'Standard AC in India is 220 V, 50 Hz (100 direction reversals/sec).', NULL, NULL, 'Standard AC in India is 220 V, 50 Hz (100 direction reversals/sec).', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a9', 'Colour of insulation on Live, Neutral, and Earth wires respectively (new international code) is:', '{"A":"Black, Red, Green","B":"Brown, Light blue, Green/Yellow","C":"Green, Red, Black","D":"Yellow, Black, Blue"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Magnetic Effects of Electric Current', 1, 'Traditional: Red/Black/Green; Standard IEC: Brown (Live), Blue (Neutral), Green-Yellow (Earth).', NULL, NULL, 'Traditional: Red/Black/Green; Standard IEC: Brown (Live), Blue (Neutral), Green-Yellow (Earth).', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000aa', 'An electric fuse is always connected in series with the:', '{"A":"Neutral wire","B":"Earth wire","C":"Live wire","D":"Any wire"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Magnetic Effects of Electric Current', 1, 'Connecting fuse on live wire isolates appliance at zero potential when blown.', NULL, NULL, 'Connecting fuse on live wire isolates appliance at zero potential when blown.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000ab', 'A positively charged alpha particle projected towards West is deflected towards North by a magnetic field. Direction of magnetic field is:', '{"A":"Towards South","B":"Towards East","C":"Downwards","D":"Upwards"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Magnetic Effects of Electric Current', 1, 'Using Fleming''s Left-Hand Rule: Current West, Force North -> Magnetic Field Upwards.', NULL, NULL, 'Using Fleming''s Left-Hand Rule: Current West, Force North -> Magnetic Field Upwards.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000ac', 'Assertion (A): Two magnetic field lines never intersect each other.
Reason (R): If they intersected, there would be two directions of magnetic field at one point, which is impossible for a compass needle.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Magnetic Effects of Electric Current', 1, 'Unique field direction prevents intersection.', NULL, NULL, 'Unique field direction prevents intersection.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000ad', 'Assertion (A): Earth wire saves the user from severe electric shock in metallic-body appliances.
Reason (R): Earth wire provides a low-resistance conducting path to ground if live wire accidentally touches the metal casing.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Magnetic Effects of Electric Current', 1, 'Low-resistance path trips the fuse and keeps casing at earth potential.', NULL, NULL, 'Low-resistance path trips the fuse and keeps casing at earth potential.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000ae', 'State Right-Hand Thumb Rule for finding magnetic field direction around a straight current-carrying wire.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Magnetic Effects of Electric Current', 2, 'Imagine holding a current-carrying straight conductor in your right hand such that the thumb points in the direction of current; then fingers wrap around the conductor in the direction of magnetic field lines.', 'Exact statement (2M)', NULL, 'Imagine holding a current-carrying straight conductor in your right hand such that the thumb points in the direction of current; then fingers wrap around the conductor in the direction of magnetic field lines.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000af', 'Differentiate between overloading and short-circuiting in domestic wiring.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Magnetic Effects of Electric Current', 2, 'Short-circuiting occurs when live and neutral wires come into direct contact (resistance drops near zero, huge current). Overloading occurs when too many high-power appliances draw current exceeding safe circuit rating.', '1M each', NULL, 'Short-circuiting occurs when live and neutral wires come into direct contact (resistance drops near zero, huge current). Overloading occurs when too many high-power appliances draw current exceeding safe circuit rating.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b0', 'What is a solenoid? List two ways to increase the magnetic field strength of an electromagnet made from a solenoid.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Magnetic Effects of Electric Current', 3, 'A coil of many circular turns of insulated copper wire wrapped closely in a cylinder shape. Field increases by: (1) increasing number of turns per unit length, (2) increasing current, or inserting a soft iron core.', 'Definition (1M) + Two ways (2M)', NULL, 'A coil of many circular turns of insulated copper wire wrapped closely in a cylinder shape. Field increases by: (1) increasing number of turns per unit length, (2) increasing current, or inserting a soft iron core.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b1', 'List three characteristic properties of magnetic field lines around a bar magnet.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Magnetic Effects of Electric Current', 3, '(1) Outside the magnet they emerge from North pole and merge at South pole; inside they run South to North (closed loops). (2) Crowding indicates stronger magnetic field near poles. (3) No two field lines intersect.', '3 × 1M', NULL, '(1) Outside the magnet they emerge from North pole and merge at South pole; inside they run South to North (closed loops). (2) Crowding indicates stronger magnetic field near poles. (3) No two field lines intersect.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b2', 'Describe an activity to demonstrate the force experienced by a current-carrying conductor placed in a magnetic field. When is this force (i) maximum and (ii) zero? State the rule used to find force direction.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Science', 'Magnetic Effects of Electric Current', 5, 'Activity: Suspend aluminium rod AB horizontally between poles of horseshoe magnet connected to battery and key. Closing key makes rod displace sideways; reversing current or magnet poles reverses displacement. Force is maximum when current is perpendicular (90°) to field and zero when parallel (0°/180°). Direction given by Fleming''s Left-Hand Rule.', 'Activity (2.5M) + Max/Zero conditions (1.5M) + Fleming Left-Hand Rule (1M)', NULL, 'Activity: Suspend aluminium rod AB horizontally between poles of horseshoe magnet connected to battery and key. Closing key makes rod displace sideways; reversing current or magnet poles reverses displacement. Force is maximum when current is perpendicular (90°) to field and zero when parallel (0°/180°). Direction given by Fleming''s Left-Hand Rule.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b3', '(i) Why did the circuit fuse blow? (ii) What is the rating of fuse needed for a 2 kW oven on 220 V? (iii) Should copper wire replace fuse wire? Give reason. (iv) Name two safety precautions during domestic use.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Science', 'Magnetic Effects of Electric Current', 4, '(i) Total current exceeded the circuit''s safe rating (overloading). (ii) I = P/V = 2000/220 ≈ 9.09 A, so a 10 A or 15 A fuse is needed. (iii) No, copper has high melting point and won''t melt quickly during faults. (iv) Use proper earthing and avoid connecting multiple high-wattage appliances in one socket.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'At home, a 2 kW microwave oven, a 1.5 kW electric iron, and a room heater are plugged together into a single 5 A extension board on a 220 V supply, immediately melting the socket fuse.', '(i) Total current exceeded the circuit''s safe rating (overloading). (ii) I = P/V = 2000/220 ≈ 9.09 A, so a 10 A or 15 A fuse is needed. (iii) No, copper has high melting point and won''t melt quickly during faults. (iv) Use proper earthing and avoid connecting multiple high-wattage appliances in one socket.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b4', 'In a terrestrial food chain, what percentage of solar energy falling on green plant leaves is converted into food energy?', '{"A":"10%","B":"1%","C":"50%","D":"25%"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Our Environment', 1, 'Green plants capture ~1% of incident sunlight; 10% law applies to subsequent trophic transfers.', NULL, NULL, 'Green plants capture ~1% of incident sunlight; 10% law applies to subsequent trophic transfers.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b5', 'If 10,000 J of energy is available at producer level, energy reaching the tertiary consumer (4th trophic level) is:', '{"A":"1000 J","B":"100 J","C":"10 J","D":"1 J"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Our Environment', 1, '10,000 -> 1,000 -> 100 -> 10 J.', NULL, NULL, '10,000 -> 1,000 -> 100 -> 10 J.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b6', 'Maximum concentration of non-biodegradable pesticides like DDT accumulates in:', '{"A":"Producers","B":"Primary consumers","C":"Top-level carnivores / humans","D":"Decomposers"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Our Environment', 1, 'Biological magnification concentrates non-biodegradable toxins at highest trophic level.', NULL, NULL, 'Biological magnification concentrates non-biodegradable toxins at highest trophic level.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b7', 'Depletion of the stratospheric ozone layer is primarily caused by:', '{"A":"Carbon dioxide","B":"Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs)","C":"Methane","D":"Nitrogen gas"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Our Environment', 1, 'CFCs release Cl radicals that break down O₃ molecules.', NULL, NULL, 'CFCs release Cl radicals that break down O₃ molecules.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b8', 'Which group contains ONLY biodegradable items?', '{"A":"Glass, Cotton, Wood","B":"Vegetable peels, Wool, Wood","C":"Plastic bottle, Paper, Polythene","D":"Aluminium foil, Grass, Leather"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Our Environment', 1, 'Vegetable peels, wool, and wood decompose via microbial action.', NULL, NULL, 'Vegetable peels, wool, and wood decompose via microbial action.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b9', 'Why is flow of energy in an ecosystem always unidirectional?', '{"A":"Decomposers return energy to the Sun","B":"Energy lost as heat at each step cannot revert to previous level","C":"Herbivores eat carnivores","D":"Sun absorbs energy from plants"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Our Environment', 1, 'Dissipated heat cannot be recycled back to lower trophic levels.', NULL, NULL, 'Dissipated heat cannot be recycled back to lower trophic levels.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000ba', 'Assertion (A): Food chains generally consist of only three or four trophic levels.
Reason (R): Due to the 10% energy transfer law, very little usable energy remains after four trophic steps.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Our Environment', 1, '90% energy loss per step caps food chain length.', NULL, NULL, '90% energy loss per step caps food chain length.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000bb', 'Assertion (A): Decomposers play no important role in maintaining soil fertility.
Reason (R): Decomposers break down dead organic waste into simple inorganic nutrients replenished into the soil.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Our Environment', 1, 'A is false; R is true.', NULL, NULL, 'A is false; R is true.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000bc', 'What is biological magnification? Why do human beings suffer the worst effects of it?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Our Environment', 2, 'Progressive increase in concentration of non-biodegradable harmful chemicals (like DDT) at each higher trophic level of a food chain. Humans sit at the top trophic level, accumulating maximum toxin concentration.', 'Definition (1M) + Human top-level reason (1M)', NULL, 'Progressive increase in concentration of non-biodegradable harmful chemicals (like DDT) at each higher trophic level of a food chain. Humans sit at the top trophic level, accumulating maximum toxin concentration.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000bd', 'How is ozone formed in the upper stratosphere? Write the chemical reactions.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Our Environment', 2, 'High-energy UV radiation splits O₂ into free oxygen atoms (O + O). Each O atom combines with O₂ to form ozone: O₂ --UV--> O + O; O + O₂ -> O₃.', 'Explanation (1M) + Equations (1M)', NULL, 'High-energy UV radiation splits O₂ into free oxygen atoms (O + O). Each O atom combines with O₂ to form ozone: O₂ --UV--> O + O; O + O₂ -> O₃.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000be', 'Differentiate between biodegradable and non-biodegradable wastes with two examples each and state one environmental hazard of non-biodegradable waste.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Our Environment', 3, 'Biodegradable: broken down naturally by microbes (fruit peels, paper). Non-biodegradable: persist for centuries without enzymatic breakdown (plastics, glass). Hazard: clogs drains, causes biomagnification, and pollutes soil/oceans.', 'Difference & examples (2M) + Hazard (1M)', NULL, 'Biodegradable: broken down naturally by microbes (fruit peels, paper). Non-biodegradable: persist for centuries without enzymatic breakdown (plastics, glass). Hazard: clogs drains, causes biomagnification, and pollutes soil/oceans.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000bf', 'Construct a 4-step aquatic food chain and explain what happens if all frogs (secondary consumers) are removed from a pond ecosystem.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Our Environment', 3, 'Phytoplankton -> Zooplankton -> Small Fish / Frog -> Crane (or Grass -> Grasshopper -> Frog -> Snake). Removing frogs increases grasshopper/insect population (damaging plants) and starves snakes.', 'Food chain (1M) + Ecosystem imbalance explanation (2M)', NULL, 'Phytoplankton -> Zooplankton -> Small Fish / Frog -> Crane (or Grass -> Grasshopper -> Frog -> Snake). Removing frogs increases grasshopper/insect population (damaging plants) and starves snakes.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000c0', 'Explain the 10% Law of energy flow in ecosystems with a labelled trophic level flow example starting from 20,000 J solar energy falling on plants. Why are artificial ecosystems like aquariums cleaned regularly while natural ponds are self-sustaining?', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Science', 'Our Environment', 5, 'Of 20,000 J solar energy, plants capture 1% = 200 J. Primary consumers get 10% = 20 J; secondary consumers get 2 J. Natural ponds have complete decomposer bacteria/fungi recycling wastes; aquariums lack sufficient natural decomposers so uneaten food and wastes accumulate, needing manual cleaning.', '10% calculation from 1% capture (3M) + Aquarium vs Pond reason (2M)', NULL, 'Of 20,000 J solar energy, plants capture 1% = 200 J. Primary consumers get 10% = 20 J; secondary consumers get 2 J. Natural ponds have complete decomposer bacteria/fungi recycling wastes; aquariums lack sufficient natural decomposers so uneaten food and wastes accumulate, needing manual cleaning.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000c1', '(i) Suggest two eco-friendly alternatives to single-use plastic cups (kulhads vs paper cups debate). (ii) Why were disposable clay kulhads later discouraged at large railway scale? (iii) Mention the two-bin waste segregation rule. (iv) Name the international treaty enacted to freeze CFC production.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Science', 'Our Environment', 4, '(i) Reusable steel tumblers or biodegradable paper cups. (ii) Mass manufacturing kulhads caused loss of fertile top clay soil. (iii) Green bin for biodegradable wet waste; Blue bin for recyclable/non-biodegradable dry waste. (iv) Montreal Protocol (UNEP, 1987).', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'A municipal corporation replaces single-use plastic tea cups across railway stations after surveys showed tons of non-decomposing plastic choking city drainage lines.', '(i) Reusable steel tumblers or biodegradable paper cups. (ii) Mass manufacturing kulhads caused loss of fertile top clay soil. (iii) Green bin for biodegradable wet waste; Blue bin for recyclable/non-biodegradable dry waste. (iv) Montreal Protocol (UNEP, 1987).', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000c2', 'When ferrous sulphate crystals (FeSO₄·7H₂O) are heated strongly in a dry boiling tube, the colour changes from green to white and then brown. Identify the gases evolved:', '{"A":"SO₂ and SO₃","B":"H₂S and O₂","C":"CO₂ and H₂","D":"NO₂ and O₂"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Chemical Reactions and Equations', 1, 'Option (A) is correct. 2FeSO₄(s) --(Heat)--> Fe₂O₃(s) + SO₂(g) + SO₃(g). Both sulphur dioxide and sulphur trioxide gases evolve with the characteristic smell of burning sulphur.', 'Correct option (A) with balanced decomposition reaction [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Remember that Fe₂O₃ is reddish-brown solid residue and two oxides of sulphur (SO₂, SO₃) are evolved. | Common Pitfall: Selecting only SO₂ or confusing with Pb(NO₃)₂ which evolves NO₂ + O₂.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000c3', 'On mixing aqueous solutions of barium chloride and sodium sulphate, a white precipitate is immediately formed. The precipitate is:', '{"A":"NaCl","B":"BaSO₄","C":"BaS","D":"Na₂S"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Chemical Reactions and Equations', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. BaCl₂(aq) + Na₂SO₄(aq) → BaSO₄(s)↓ + 2NaCl(aq). Barium sulphate (BaSO₄) is insoluble in water and settles as a white precipitate.', 'Identify BaSO₄ as insoluble white precipitate [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Double displacement reactions involving Ba²⁺ and SO₄²⁻ ions yield insoluble BaSO₄. | Common Pitfall: Writing NaCl as the precipitate.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000c4', 'During the electrolysis of brine (aqueous NaCl), which products are formed at the anode and cathode respectively?', '{"A":"Cl₂ at anode, H₂ at cathode","B":"H₂ at anode, Cl₂ at cathode","C":"NaOH at anode, Cl₂ at cathode","D":"O₂ at anode, Na at cathode"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Acids, Bases and Salts', 1, 'Option (A) is correct. In the chlor-alkali process: 2NaCl(aq) + 2H₂O(l) → 2NaOH(aq) + Cl₂(g) [at anode] + H₂(g) [at cathode].', 'Option (A) identifying Cl₂ at anode (+) and H₂ at cathode (-) [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Negative Cl⁻ ions migrate to positive Anode; positive H⁺ ions migrate to negative Cathode. | Common Pitfall: Swapping anode and cathode gases.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000c5', 'Which of the following salts does NOT contain water of crystallisation?', '{"A":"Blue vitriol","B":"Baking soda","C":"Washing soda","D":"Gypsum"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Acids, Bases and Salts', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. Baking soda is anhydrous sodium hydrogencarbonate (NaHCO₃). Blue vitriol (CuSO₄·5H₂O), washing soda (Na₂CO₃·10H₂O), and gypsum (CaSO₄·2H₂O) contain water of crystallisation.', 'Identify NaHCO₃ (baking soda) as anhydrous [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Memorize formulas of all 4 hydrated salts from NCERT Chapter 2. | Common Pitfall: Confusing baking soda (NaHCO₃) with washing soda (Na₂CO₃·10H₂O).', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000c6', 'An element X reacts with oxygen to form an oxide X₂O₃ which reacts with both HCl and NaOH to produce salt and water. The element X is:', '{"A":"Sodium","B":"Magnesium","C":"Aluminium","D":"Sulphur"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Metals and Non-metals', 1, 'Option (C) is correct. Aluminium forms aluminium oxide (Al₂O₃), which is amphoteric — it reacts with both acids and bases to yield salt and water.', 'Option (C) identifying Al₂O₃ as amphoteric oxide [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: The two standard amphoteric oxides in Class 10 CBSE are Al₂O₃ and ZnO. | Common Pitfall: Choosing basic oxides like Na₂O or MgO.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000c7', 'The reaction Fe₂O₃(s) + 2Al(s) → 2Fe(l) + Al₂O₃(s) + Heat is used to join cracked railway tracks because:', '{"A":"It is endothermic and cools the track","B":"It is highly exothermic producing molten iron","C":"Aluminium oxide melts into steel","D":"Iron is more reactive than aluminium"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Metals and Non-metals', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. The thermit reaction is a highly exothermic displacement reaction where Al reduces Fe₂O₃ and the heat released melts the iron produced.', 'Option (B) noting exothermic nature yielding molten iron [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Always mention molten state of Fe(l) due to intense heat. | Common Pitfall: Thinking Fe displaces Al instead of Al displacing Fe.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000c8', 'Identify the functional group present in CH₃–CO–CH₃ (Propanone):', '{"A":"Aldehyde (-CHO)","B":"Ketone (>C=O)","C":"Carboxylic acid (-COOH)","D":"Alcohol (-OH)"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Carbon and its Compounds', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. Propanone contains the carbonyl group (>C=O) bonded to two carbon atoms on either side, characteristic of ketones.', 'Identify ketone functional group (>C=O) [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Suffix "-one" always represents Ketone; "-al" represents Aldehyde. | Common Pitfall: Confusing propanone with propanal.', 'EASY', true)
        ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

Reason (R): Carbon is oxidised to CO while ZnO is reduced to Zn simultaneously.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Chemical Reactions', 1, 'Option (A) is correct. Carbon gains oxygen (oxidation) and ZnO loses oxygen (reduction) simultaneously, proving it is a redox reaction.', 'Option (A) with oxidation and reduction explanation [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Check truth of A, truth of R, and causal link. | Common Pitfall: Confusing oxidising agent (ZnO) with substance oxidised (C).', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000d3', 'Assertion (A): The sex of a newborn human child is determined genetically by the father.
Reason (R): All children inherit an X chromosome from their mother regardless of whether they are boys or girls.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Heredity', 1, 'Option (A) is correct. Mother is XX (always contributes X). Father is XY (contributes X or Y). Thus inheriting X or Y from father decides XX (girl) or XY (boy).', 'Option (A) with XX/XY chromosome inheritance proof [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Equal 50% probability exists for male and female child. | Common Pitfall: Selecting (B) when R directly explains why mother does not decide sex.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000d4', 'Assertion (A): Domestic electrical appliances in a house are connected in parallel rather than series.
Reason (R): In a parallel circuit, each appliance receives the full mains voltage and has an independent switch.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Electricity', 1, 'Option (A) is correct. Parallel connection ensures constant 220 V across every appliance, lower total resistance, and independent operation.', 'Option (A) explaining independent switching and constant voltage [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: In series, if one bulb fuses, the entire circuit breaks. | Common Pitfall: Thinking parallel wiring increases total circuit resistance.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000d5', 'Assertion (A): Maximum concentration of non-biodegradable pesticides like DDT accumulates in human beings at the top of the food chain.
Reason (R): Pesticides get degraded rapidly at every successive trophic level.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Our Environment', 1, 'Option (C) is correct. Assertion A is true (biomagnification), but Reason R is false because non-biodegradable pesticides are NOT degraded or washed away — they accumulate progressively.', 'Option (C): A true, R false [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Read words like "degraded rapidly" carefully — non-biodegradable chemicals persist. | Common Pitfall: Selecting (A) without catching the contradiction in R.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000d6', '2 g of silver bromide is taken in a china dish and kept in sunlight for some time.
(i) State the colour change observed.
(ii) Write the balanced chemical equation and mention one practical use of this reaction.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Chemical Reactions', 2, '(i) Pale yellow silver bromide turns grey due to formation of metallic silver.
(ii) 2AgBr(s) --(Sunlight)--> 2Ag(s) + Br₂(g). Use: Black and white photography.', '(i) Colour change pale yellow to grey [0.5 Mark] | (ii) Balanced equation with Sunlight above arrow [1 Mark] | Application in B&W photography [0.5 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Always write "Sunlight" over the reaction arrow for photolytic decomposition. | Common Pitfall: Writing AgCl instead of AgBr or forgetting to balance Br₂.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000d7', 'Why is it advised to add concentrated sulphuric acid slowly to water with constant stirring rather than adding water to acid?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Acids, Bases and Salts', 2, 'Dilution of concentrated acid is a highly exothermic process. If water is added to concentrated acid, the large heat generated instantly converts water to steam, causing the acidic mixture to splash out and cause severe burns. Adding acid slowly to water allows water to absorb the heat safely.', 'Highly exothermic nature of acid dilution [1 Mark] | Risk of splashing/burns vs safe heat absorption [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Mention "highly exothermic" and "splash out / container may break". | Common Pitfall: Saying acid explodes chemically with water.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000d8', '(a) Why are rings of cartilage present in the human trachea?
(b) What is the importance of residual volume of air in lungs?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Life Processes', 2, '(a) C-shaped rings of cartilage ensure that the air passage (trachea) does not collapse even when there is very little air in it.
(b) Residual volume ensures there is always sufficient time for oxygen to be absorbed and carbon dioxide to be released during the breathing cycle.', '(a) Prevent collapse of trachea [1 Mark] | (b) Sufficient time for O2 absorption & CO2 release [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Quote NCERT directly for residual volume function. | Common Pitfall: Stating cartilage filters dust instead of preventing collapse.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000d9', 'How does our body respond when blood sugar level rises sharply after a meal? Name the hormone, the gland secreting it, and the feedback mechanism involved.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Control and Coordination', 2, 'Cells of the pancreas detect the rise in blood glucose and secrete more insulin hormone. Insulin stimulates body cells and liver to absorb glucose and convert excess into glycogen, lowering blood sugar. Once sugar drops to normal, insulin secretion decreases via feedback mechanism.', 'Pancreas + Insulin identification [1 Mark] | Feedback regulation mechanism explained [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Always name both Pancreas (gland) and Insulin (hormone). | Common Pitfall: Writing adrenaline or thyroxine.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000da', 'Why do drivers prefer a convex mirror over a plane or concave mirror as a rear-view mirror in vehicles? Give two reasons.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Light', 2, '1. A convex mirror always forms a virtual, erect, and diminished image regardless of object distance.
2. It has a much wider field of view because it is curved outwards, allowing the driver to see a large area of traffic behind.', 'Always forms erect image [1 Mark] | Wider field of view [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Write both points cleanly as numbered bullets. | Common Pitfall: Confusing rear-view mirror (convex) with headlight reflector (concave).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000db', 'What is ozone? How is it formed in the upper stratosphere, and which man-made chemicals caused its sharp depletion in the 1980s?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Our Environment', 2, 'Ozone (O₃) is a triatomic molecule of oxygen that shields Earth from harmful UV radiation.
Formation: UV rays split O₂ into free oxygen atoms (O), which combine with O₂: O₂ --(UV)--> O + O; O + O₂ → O₃.
Depleting agents: Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) used in refrigerants and fire extinguishers.', 'UV splitting O₂ + O + O₂ -> O₃ [1 Mark] | CFCs named as depleting chemical [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Include both chemical equations for O₃ formation. | Common Pitfall: Writing CO₂ or SO₂ instead of CFCs.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000dc', '(a) Write the chemical formula and preparation equation of Plaster of Paris from gypsum.
(b) Why should Plaster of Paris be stored in a moisture-proof container?
(c) Give one medical and one decorative use of Plaster of Paris.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Acids, Bases and Salts', 3, '(a) Formula: CaSO₄·½H₂O (Calcium sulphate hemihydrate).
Preparation: CaSO₄·2H₂O --(373 K)--> CaSO₄·½H₂O + 1½H₂O.
(b) On absorbing moisture, it reacts to set into a hard solid mass (gypsum), becoming useless.
(c) Medical: Supporting fractured bones in right position; Decorative: Making toys/statues/smooth ceilings.', '(a) Formula + balanced 373 K equation [1.5 Marks] | (b) Sets into hard gypsum with moisture [1 Mark] | (c) Two uses [0.5 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Always specify temperature 373 K (100°C); above this dead burnt plaster forms. | Common Pitfall: Writing CaSO₄·2H₂O as Plaster of Paris instead of gypsum.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000dd', 'Explain the electrolytic refining of impure copper with a well-labelled description of:
(a) Anode, Cathode, and Electrolyte used
(b) What happens to soluble and insoluble impurities (anode mud)?', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Metals and Non-metals', 3, '(a) Anode: Thick block of impure copper; Cathode: Thin strip of pure copper; Electrolyte: Acidified aqueous copper sulphate (CuSO₄) solution.
(b) On passing current, pure copper dissolves from anode into electrolyte and deposits on cathode. Soluble impurities pass into solution, while insoluble impurities (Ag, Au) settle below the anode as anode mud.', '(a) Correct anode, cathode, and electrolyte [1.5 Marks] | (b) Ion migration + anode mud explanation [1.5 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Remember: Impure metal is ALWAYS Anode; Pure thin strip is ALWAYS Cathode. | Common Pitfall: Swapping impure and pure electrodes.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000de', 'Explain the three main steps involved in urine formation inside a nephron of the human kidney.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Life Processes', 3, '1. Glomerular Filtration: Blood enters glomerulus under high pressure; water, urea, glucose, amino acids and salts filter into Bowman''s capsule.
2. Selective Reabsorption: As filtrate flows along renal tubule, useful substances like glucose, amino acids, salts and major amount of water are selectively reabsorbed into surrounding capillaries.
3. Secretion / Collection: Remaining nitrogenous waste (urea) and excess water enter collecting duct as urine.', 'Ultrafiltration in Bowman''s capsule [1 Mark] | Selective reabsorption of glucose/water [1.5 Marks] | Amount of water reabsorbed depends on body water/waste [0.5 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Mention what determines how much water is reabsorbed: excess water in body and dissolved waste amount. | Common Pitfall: Forgetting to list selective reabsorption of glucose and amino acids.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000df', 'An object 4 cm in height is placed at 25 cm in front of a concave mirror of focal length 15 cm. At what distance from the mirror should a screen be placed to obtain a sharp image? Find the nature and height of the image.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Light', 3, 'Given: h = +4 cm, u = -25 cm, f = -15 cm.
Mirror formula: 1/f = 1/v + 1/u => 1/(-15) = 1/v + 1/(-25)
1/v = -1/15 + 1/25 = (-5 + 3)/75 = -2/75 => v = -37.5 cm.
Screen should be placed 37.5 cm in front of the mirror.
Magnification m = h''/h = -v/u => h''/4 = -(-37.5)/(-25) = -1.5 => h'' = -6.0 cm.
Nature: Real, inverted and enlarged (height 6 cm).', 'New Cartesian signs (u=-25, f=-15) & formula [1 Mark] | Image distance v = -37.5 cm [1 Mark] | Height h'' = -6 cm & nature (real, inverted) [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Verify with ray case: object between F (15 cm) and C (30 cm) -> image beyond C (37.5 cm), real, inverted, enlarged. | Common Pitfall: Taking u or f as positive for a concave mirror.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000e0', 'A student sitting on the last bench cannot read the blackboard clearly, though he can read his textbook comfortably.
(a) Name the defect of vision and state its two causes.
(b) Which lens corrects this defect? If his far point is 80 cm, calculate the power of the corrective lens required.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Human Eye', 3, '(a) Defect: Myopia (near-sightedness). Causes: (i) Excessive curvature of the eye lens; (ii) Elongation of the eyeball.
(b) Correction: Concave (diverging) lens.
For u = -∞, v = -80 cm = -0.8 m.
1/f = 1/v - 1/u = 1/(-0.8) - 0 = -1.25 D.
Power P = -1.25 D.', '(a) Myopia + two anatomical causes [1.5 Marks] | (b) Concave lens + correct sign numerical P = -1.25 D [1.5 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Always convert cm to metres before computing Power P = 1/f(m). | Common Pitfall: Using positive focal length for myopia correction.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000e1', 'Three resistors of 5 Ω, 10 Ω, and 30 Ω are connected in parallel across a 12 V battery. Calculate:
(a) Current through each resistor
(b) Total current drawn from the battery
(c) Total effective resistance of the circuit.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Electricity', 3, '(a) Voltage across each branch is V = 12 V:
I₁ = 12 / 5 = 2.4 A
I₂ = 12 / 10 = 1.2 A
I₃ = 12 / 30 = 0.4 A
(b) Total current I = I₁ + I₂ + I₃ = 2.4 + 1.2 + 0.4 = 4.0 A.
(c) 1/Rp = 1/5 + 1/10 + 1/30 = (6 + 3 + 1)/30 = 10/30 = 1/3 => Rp = 3 Ω (or V/I = 12/4 = 3 Ω).', '(a) Branch currents 2.4 A, 1.2 A, 0.4 A [1 Mark] | (b) Total current 4.0 A [1 Mark] | (c) Effective resistance 3 Ω [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Check: Rp (3 Ω) must always be smaller than the smallest individual resistor (5 Ω). | Common Pitfall: Forgetting to reciprocate 1/Rp = 1/3 back to Rp = 3 Ω.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000e2', 'List three different categories of contraceptive methods used to prevent unwanted pregnancies, giving one example of each. Which method also protects against sexually transmitted diseases (STDs)?', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'How do Organisms Reproduce', 3, '1. Mechanical / Barrier methods: Condoms (male/female) or cervical caps — prevent sperm from reaching egg.
2. Chemical / Hormonal methods: Oral contraceptive pills — alter hormonal balance to prevent ovulation.
3. Intra-Uterine Devices (IUDs) / Surgical methods: Copper-T (IUD) or Vasectomy/Tubectomy.
STD Protection: Barrier method (Condoms) provides protection against STDs such as HIV-AIDS, gonorrhoea, and syphilis.', 'Three distinct categories with 1 example each [2 Marks] | Condoms/barrier method protects from STDs [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Highlight that hormonal pills can cause side effects if taken carelessly. | Common Pitfall: Saying IUDs or pills protect against STDs.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000e3', '(a) Complete the following chemical equations:
  (i) CH₃COOH + C₂H₅OH --(Conc. H₂SO₄)--> ?
  (ii) CH₃CH₂OH --(Alkaline KMnO₄ + Heat)--> ?
  (iii) CH₃COOC₂H₅ + NaOH → ?
(b) Draw the electron dot structure of Ethanoic acid or Ethyne (C₂H₂).
(c) Explain why soaps do not form lather easily in hard water.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Science', 'Carbon and its Compounds', 5, '(a) (i) CH₃COOC₂H₅ (Ethyl ethanoate ester) + H₂O (Esterification).
(ii) CH₃COOH (Ethanoic acid) + H₂O (Oxidation).
(iii) CH₃COONa (Sodium ethanoate) + C₂H₅OH (Saponification).
(b) Ethyne: H : C ⋮⋮ C : H (each carbon shares 3 electron pairs with other carbon and 1 pair with H).
(c) Hard water contains Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ ions, which react with soap molecules (sodium salts of fatty acids) to form an insoluble curdy white precipitate called scum, wasting soap.', '(a) Three balanced equations with product names [3 Marks] | (b) Correct covalent electron dot structure [1 Mark] | (c) Ca²⁺/Mg²⁺ scum formation explanation [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Write IUPAC names below products in organic equations. | Common Pitfall: Confusing esterification (acid + alcohol) with saponification (ester + NaOH).', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000e4', '(a) Name the enzyme present in (i) Saliva, (ii) Gastric juice, (iii) Pancreatic juice (for protein) and state their substrates and products.
(b) How is the small intestine structurally adapted for maximum absorption of digested food?
(c) Why do herbivores have a longer small intestine than carnivores?', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Science', 'Life Processes', 5, '(a) (i) Salivary amylase: Starch → Maltose (simple sugar).
(ii) Pepsin (active in acidic HCl): Proteins → Peptones.
(iii) Trypsin (active in alkaline medium): Proteins → Simpler peptides/amino acids.
(b) Inner lining of small intestine has millions of finger-like projections called villi, which increase surface area immensely and are richly supplied with blood vessels to transport absorbed nutrients.
(c) Herbivores eat grass rich in cellulose, which takes much longer to digest; carnivores eat meat which is easier to digest, hence shorter small intestine.', '(a) All 3 enzymes, substrates, products [3 Marks] | (b) Villi + rich blood supply [1 Mark] | (c) Cellulose digestion in herbivores [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Mention acidic medium for Pepsin and alkaline medium for Trypsin. | Common Pitfall: Saying pepsin digests starch or trypsin works in stomach.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000e5', '(a) State Ohm''s law and derive the relation for equivalent resistance of two resistors R₁ and R₂ connected in parallel.
(b) Two lamps rated 100 W at 220 V and 60 W at 220 V are connected in parallel to a 220 V supply. Calculate the total current drawn from the line.
(c) Why is tungsten used almost exclusively for filament of electric lamps?', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Science', 'Electricity', 5, '(a) Ohm''s Law: At constant temperature, current I flowing through a metallic conductor is directly proportional to potential difference V across its ends (V = IR).
Parallel derivation: I = I₁ + I₂ => V/Rp = V/R₁ + V/R₂ => 1/Rp = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂.
(b) Total power in parallel P = 100 + 60 = 160 W.
Current I = P / V = 160 / 220 = 8/11 ≈ 0.727 A.
(c) Tungsten has a very high melting point (3380°C) and high resistivity, so it glows white-hot without melting.', '(a) Ohm''s law + 1/Rp derivation [2 Marks] | (b) P = 160 W and I = 0.73 A [2 Marks] | (c) High melting point 3380°C of tungsten [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: In parallel, powers simply add up: P_total = P₁ + P₂. | Common Pitfall: Forgetting "at constant physical conditions / temperature" in Ohm''s law.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000e6', 'Read the passage and answer the questions:
A student tested four aqueous solutions P, Q, R, and S with universal indicator paper. Solution P turned dark red (pH = 1), Q turned orange (pH = 4), R turned green (pH = 7), and S turned dark violet (pH = 13).

(a) Arrange P, Q, R, S in increasing order of hydrogen ion [H⁺] concentration. (1M)
(b) Identify which solution can be used to treat acidity in the stomach or neutralize acidic soil, and why. (1M)
(c) What happens when solid sodium carbonate is added to solution P? Write the balanced chemical equation assuming P is HCl. (2M)', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Science', 'Case Study', 4, '(a) Increasing [H⁺]: S (pH 13) < R (pH 7) < Q (pH 4) < P (pH 1).
(b) Solution S (strong base, or mild bases like Mg(OH)₂/CaO) neutralizes excess H⁺ ions, though mild antacids like milk of magnesia (pH ~10) are preferred internally.
(c) Brisk effervescence of CO₂ gas occurs, which turns lime water milky.
Equation: Na₂CO₃(s) + 2HCl(aq) → 2NaCl(aq) + H₂O(l) + CO₂(g).', '(a) Correct order S < R < Q < P [1 Mark] | (b) Neutralisation logic [1 Mark] | (c) Effervescence + balanced Na₂CO₃ + 2HCl equation [2 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Higher pH means LOWER H⁺ concentration! | Common Pitfall: Arranging pH values directly as H⁺ order (reversing the inequality).', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000e7', 'Read the passage and answer the questions:
When a ray of light travels obliquely from air (n = 1.0) into crown glass (n = 1.52) and diamond (n = 2.42), its speed decreases depending on the optical density of the medium. Lenses made of glass are used in cameras, projectors, and spectacles.

(a) State Snell''s Law of refraction. (1M)
(b) In which medium (crown glass or diamond) is the speed of light lower, and by what ratio? (1M)
(c) If the speed of light in vacuum is 3 × 10⁸ m/s and refractive index of water is 4/3, calculate the speed of light in water. Also state what happens to lateral displacement if thickness of a glass slab increases. (2M)', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Science', 'Case Study', 4, '(a) Snell''s Law: Ratio of sine of angle of incidence to sine of angle of refraction is constant for a given colour of light and pair of media (sin i / sin r = n).
(b) Speed is lower in diamond (higher refractive index n = 2.42); ratio v_glass / v_diamond = 2.42 / 1.52 ≈ 1.59.
(c) v_water = c / n = (3 × 10⁸) / (4/3) = 2.25 × 10⁸ m/s. Lateral displacement INCREASES directly as slab thickness increases.', '(a) Snell''s law statement/formula [1 Mark] | (b) Diamond has lower speed [1 Mark] | (c) v = 2.25 × 10⁸ m/s + direct relation with thickness [2 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: v = c / n — refractive index is inversely proportional to speed of light. | Common Pitfall: Multiplying c by n instead of dividing c by n.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000e8', 'Read the passage and answer the questions:
Riya set up an electric circuit with a 6 V battery, two resistors R₁ = 4 Ω and R₂ = 8 Ω connected in series, an ammeter, and a voltmeter across R₁.

(a) Calculate the total resistance and ammeter reading in the circuit. (1M)
(b) What will be the voltmeter reading across R₁ (4 Ω)? (1M)
(c) Calculate the heat dissipated in R₂ (8 Ω) in 2 minutes. (2M)', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Science', 'Case Study', 4, '(a) Rs = R₁ + R₂ = 4 + 8 = 12 Ω. Current I = V / Rs = 6 / 12 = 0.5 A.
(b) Voltmeter reading V₁ = I × R₁ = 0.5 × 4 = 2.0 V.
(c) Time t = 2 min = 120 s. Heat H = I² R₂ t = (0.5)² × 8 × 120 = 0.25 × 8 × 120 = 240 J.', '(a) Rs = 12 Ω, I = 0.5 A [1 Mark] | (b) V₁ = 2 V [1 Mark] | (c) H = I²Rt with t = 120 s = 240 J [2 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Always convert minutes to SI unit seconds (2 min = 120 s). | Common Pitfall: Using t = 2 instead of 120 s in Joule''s formula.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000e9', 'A cylindrical copper wire is stretched to double its length without changing its volume. Its electrical resistivity (ρ) will:', '{"A":"Become 4 times","B":"Become double","C":"Become half","D":"Remain unchanged"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Electricity', 1, 'Option (D) is correct. Resistivity (ρ) is a characteristic property of the material and temperature; it does NOT depend on length or cross-sectional area (unlike resistance R, which becomes 4 times).', 'Resistivity depends only on material nature and temperature [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Read carefully: question asks RESISTIVITY, not resistance! | Common Pitfall: Calculating R'' = 4R and marking Option (A).', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000ea', 'An electric iron of power rating 1100 W is operated at 220 V. Which fuse rating is most appropriate for this appliance?', '{"A":"1 A","B":"3 A","C":"5 A","D":"6 A or higher (e.g. 10 A)"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Electricity', 1, 'Option (D) is correct. Normal current I = P / V = 1100 / 220 = 5 A. A fuse must have a rating slightly above the normal operating current (e.g., 6 A / 10 A) so it does not melt during normal operation.', 'Calculate I = 5 A and select rating slightly above 5 A [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Never choose a 5 A fuse when operating current is 5 A; slight surge will blow it. | Common Pitfall: Marking 5 A directly without accounting for fuse margin.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000eb', 'The magnetic field lines inside a long straight current-carrying solenoid are:', '{"A":"Concentric circles","B":"Parallel straight lines indicating uniform magnetic field","C":"Radial outward lines","D":"Zero everywhere inside"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Magnetic Effects', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. Inside a current-carrying solenoid, the magnetic field lines are parallel and equidistant straight lines, showing that the magnetic field is uniform at all points inside.', 'Parallel straight lines = uniform magnetic field inside solenoid [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Remember NCERT phrase: "field lines are in the form of parallel straight lines". | Common Pitfall: Confusing solenoid interior with straight conductor circular field.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000ec', 'Assertion (A): Two magnetic field lines never intersect each other at any point.
Reason (R): If they intersected, a magnetic compass needle placed at that point would point in two different directions simultaneously, which is impossible.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Magnetic Effects', 1, 'Option (A) is correct. Tangent to a field line gives the unique direction of net magnetic force at a point; two directions at one point cannot exist.', 'Option (A) with compass needle contradiction logic [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Standard 1M/2M board favourite question. | Common Pitfall: Confusing field lines with electric current paths.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000ed', 'Assertion (A): Saturated hydrocarbons normally burn with a clean blue flame in sufficient oxygen.
Reason (R): Saturated hydrocarbons contain a higher percentage of carbon than unsaturated hydrocarbons of the same chain length.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Science', 'Carbon Compounds', 1, 'Option (C) is correct. Saturated hydrocarbons burn with a clean flame because their percentage of carbon is LOWER than unsaturated hydrocarbons (alkenes/alkynes), allowing complete combustion.', 'Option (C): A true, R false [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Unsaturated hydrocarbons have higher C:H mass ratio -> yellow sooty flame. | Common Pitfall: Selecting (A) by overlooking "higher percentage".', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000ee', 'Give reasons:
(i) Sodium catches fire when dropped in cold water, whereas calcium floats without catching fire.
(ii) Aluminium does not corrode easily even though it is high in the reactivity series.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Metals and Non-metals', 2, '(i) Na reacts violently with cold water and the exothermic heat ignites the evolved H₂ gas. Calcium reacts less violently (heat insufficient to ignite H₂), and bubbles of H₂ stick to its surface making it float.
(ii) Aluminium forms a thin, tough protective layer of Al₂O₃ on its surface that prevents further oxidation.', '(i) Na exothermic H2 ignition vs Ca bubbles sticking [1 Mark] | (ii) Protective Al₂O₃ layer [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Mention "H₂ bubbles stick to calcium surface" explicitly. | Common Pitfall: Saying calcium is lighter than water by density.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000ef', 'Why are coils of electric toasters and electric irons made of an alloy (nichrome) rather than a pure metal like copper?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'Electricity', 2, '1. Alloys have much higher resistivity than their constituent pure metals, producing substantial Joule heat (H = I²Rt).
2. Alloys do not oxidise (burn) readily at high red-hot temperatures.', 'Higher resistivity of alloys [1 Mark] | Does not oxidise/burn easily at high temperature [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Both points are strictly required in CBSE marking schemes. | Common Pitfall: Writing "high melting point" alone without mentioning non-oxidation.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000f0', 'In a cross between pea plants having Round Yellow seeds (RRYY) and Wrinkled Green seeds (rryy), what type of seeds appear in F₁? State the F₂ phenotypic ratio and explain what it proves.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Heredity', 3, 'F₁ generation: All plants produce Round Yellow seeds (RrYy) because Round (R) and Yellow (Y) are dominant traits.
F₂ phenotypic ratio: 9 (Round Yellow) : 3 (Round Green) : 3 (Wrinkled Yellow) : 1 (Wrinkled Green).
Conclusion: It proves the Law of Independent Assortment — seed shape and seed colour traits are inherited independently of each other.', 'F1 all Round Yellow (RrYy) [1 Mark] | F2 ratio 9:3:3:1 with labels [1 Mark] | Independent inheritance of traits [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: New recombinant combinations (Round Green & Wrinkled Yellow) prove independent assortment. | Common Pitfall: Writing 3:1 monohybrid ratio instead of 9:3:3:1.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000f1', '(a) State Fleming''s Left-Hand Rule.
(b) An electron enters a magnetic field directed from North to South while moving vertically downwards. Find the direction of force on the electron.
(c) Mention the colour coding of Live, Neutral, and Earth wires in India.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Science', 'Magnetic Effects', 3, '(a) Stretch thumb, forefinger and middle finger of left hand mutually perpendicular: Forefinger = Magnetic Field (B), Middle finger = Current (I), Thumb = Force/Motion (F).
(b) Electron moves downwards => conventional current is vertically UPWARDS. Field is N→S. Applying Left-Hand Rule gives force towards WEST (or East depending on viewer frame; specifically perpendicular to both: let down=-y, N->S=-z => I=+y, B=-z => F = I×B = +y×(-z) = -x (West)).
(c) Live = Red, Neutral = Black, Earth = Green.', '(a) Correct Fleming Left-Hand Rule statement [1 Mark] | (b) Reversing electron direction for current -> force direction [1 Mark] | (c) Red, Black, Green wire colours [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Always reverse direction of negative charges (electrons) to get conventional current! | Common Pitfall: Taking electron velocity directly as conventional current direction.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000f2', '(a) Draw ray diagrams for image formation by a convex lens when an object is placed: (i) between F₁ and 2F₁, (ii) between optical centre O and F₁.
(b) A concave lens of focal length 15 cm forms an image 10 cm from the lens. How far is the object placed from the lens? Also compute magnification.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Science', 'Light', 5, '(a) (i) Object between F₁ and 2F₁: Real, inverted, magnified image beyond 2F₂.
(ii) Object between O and F₁: Virtual, erect, magnified image on same side as object (magnifying glass).
(b) Concave lens: f = -15 cm, v = -10 cm.
Lens formula: 1/f = 1/v - 1/u => 1/u = 1/v - 1/f = 1/(-10) - 1/(-15) = -1/10 + 1/15 = -1/30.
Hence u = -30 cm (object is 30 cm in front of lens).
Magnification m = v/u = (-10)/(-30) = +1/3 ≈ +0.33 (virtual, erect, diminished).', '(a) Both ray diagram positions & image natures [2 Marks] | (b) Lens formula + u = -30 cm [2 Marks] | Magnification m = +1/3 [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: For concave lens, u, v, and f are ALL negative! | Common Pitfall: Using mirror formula (1/v + 1/u) instead of lens formula (1/v - 1/u).', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000f3', 'Read the passage and answer the questions:
The human heart has four chambers that prevent mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood, ensuring a highly efficient oxygen supply needed for warm-blooded mammals and birds.

(a) Why do birds and mammals require complete separation of left and right sides of the heart? (1M)
(b) Why do ventricles have thicker muscular walls than atria? (1M)
(c) Differentiate between arteries and veins based on wall thickness, valves, and blood direction. (2M)', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Science', 'Case Study', 4, '(a) Birds and mammals are warm-blooded and constantly use high energy to maintain steady body temperature, requiring uninterrupted oxygen-rich blood.
(b) Ventricles have to pump blood under high pressure to distant organs and lungs, whereas atria only push blood into adjacent ventricles.
(c) Arteries: Thick elastic walls, no valves, carry blood away from heart under high pressure. Veins: Thin walls, have valves to prevent backflow, collect blood from organs back to heart.', '(a) High energy for constant body temperature [1 Mark] | (b) Ventricles pump blood to distant organs [1 Mark] | (c) 2 clear differences between arteries and veins [2 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Mention "maintain constant body temperature" for warm-blooded animals. | Common Pitfall: Saying arteries always carry oxygenated blood without noting pulmonary artery exception.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000f4', 'In a terrestrial food chain Grass → Deer → Lion, if 5000 J of solar energy is captured by grass as chemical energy, how much energy is available to the lion?', '{"A":"500 J","B":"50 J","C":"5 J","D":"0.5 J"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Our Environment', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. According to Lindeman''s 10% law: Grass (5000 J) → Deer (10% = 500 J) → Lion (10% of 500 = 50 J).', 'Apply 10% rule twice: 5000 -> 500 -> 50 J [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Count trophic level steps carefully: 2 transfers = divide by 100. | Common Pitfall: Applying 10% from sunlight (1%) instead of energy already trapped by plants.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000f5', 'The green coating developed on copper vessels exposed to moist air is chemically:', '{"A":"Copper sulphide (CuS)","B":"Basic copper carbonate [CuCO₃·Cu(OH)₂]","C":"Copper oxide (CuO)","D":"Copper nitrate"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Chemical Reactions', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. Copper reacts slowly with moist CO₂ in air to lose its shiny brown surface and gain a green coat of basic copper carbonate.', 'Identify basic copper carbonate as green coating [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Silver -> black Ag₂S; Copper -> green basic copper carbonate; Iron -> reddish-brown Fe₂O₃·xH₂O. | Common Pitfall: Confusing green coating on copper with black CuO formed on heating.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000f6', 'Even though bile juice contains no digestive enzymes, it is essential for digestion in the small intestine because it:', '{"A":"Digests starch into maltose","B":"Makes acidic food alkaline and emulsifies large fat globules","C":"Breaks proteins into amino acids","D":"Absorbs water from undigested food"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Life Processes', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. Bile salts make the acidic chyme from stomach alkaline for pancreatic enzymes to act, and emulsify large fat globules into tiny droplets increasing surface area for lipase.', 'Alkaline medium + emulsification of fats [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Use the keyword "emulsification of fats" for full credit. | Common Pitfall: Writing that bile directly digests fats into fatty acids.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000f7', 'Define placenta and state two vital functions it performs for the developing human embryo inside the uterus.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Science', 'How do Organisms Reproduce', 2, 'Placenta is a special disc-shaped tissue embedded in the uterine wall containing villi on the embryo''s side and blood spaces on the mother''s side.
Functions: (1) Transfers glucose and oxygen from mother''s blood to embryo; (2) Removes metabolic wastes generated by the embryo into mother''s blood.', 'Structure/definition with villi [1 Mark] | Nutrient/O2 supply & waste removal [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Highlight the huge surface area provided by villi. | Common Pitfall: Saying mother and baby blood mixes directly.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000f8', '(a) State the functions of: (i) Ovary, (ii) Fallopian tube (Oviduct), (iii) Uterus in the human female reproductive system.
(b) What happens in the uterus if the egg is NOT fertilised?
(c) Trace the events following pollination inside a bisexual flower up to seed formation.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Science', 'How do Organisms Reproduce', 5, '(a) (i) Ovary: Produces female gametes (ova/eggs) and hormones oestrogen & progesterone.
(ii) Oviduct: Site of fertilisation and transports egg to uterus.
(iii) Uterus: Site of implantation and embryo development.
(b) If egg is not fertilised, the thick spongy uterine lining breaks down and exits through vagina as blood and mucus (menstruation, ~2-8 days).
(c) Pollen lands on stigma -> pollen tube grows through style to ovule -> male germ cell fuses with female egg cell (fertilisation) forming zygote -> zygote forms embryo, ovule becomes seed with tough coat, ovary ripens into fruit.', '(a) Functions of ovary, oviduct, uterus [2 Marks] | (b) Menstruation breakdown explained [1 Mark] | (c) Pollen tube -> fertilisation -> ovule=seed, ovary=fruit [2 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Remember: Ovule -> Seed; Ovary -> Fruit. | Common Pitfall: Writing fertilisation occurs in the uterus instead of oviduct.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000f9', 'Read the passage and answer the questions:
A farmer crossed pure violet-flowered pea plants (VV) with pure white-flowered pea plants (vv). In the F₁ generation, all 120 plants produced violet flowers. He then self-pollinated the F₁ plants to raise 800 plants in F₂.

(a) State the genotype of all F₁ plants and identify the recessive trait. (1M)
(b) How many plants out of 800 in F₂ are expected to have white flowers? (1M)
(c) Out of 800 F₂ plants, how many are expected to be pure homozygous violet (VV) vs heterozygous violet (Vv)? (2M)', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Science', 'Case Study', 4, '(a) Genotype of F₁: Vv (heterozygous violet). White flower colour (v) is recessive.
(b) F₂ phenotypic ratio is 3:1 => White flowers = 1/4 × 800 = 200 plants.
(c) F₂ genotypic ratio is 1 VV : 2 Vv : 1 vv. Pure homozygous VV = 1/4 × 800 = 200 plants; Heterozygous Vv = 2/4 × 800 = 400 plants.', '(a) Vv genotype + white is recessive [1 Mark] | (b) 200 white plants [1 Mark] | (c) 200 VV and 400 Vv [2 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Check sum: 200 (VV) + 400 (Vv) + 200 (vv) = 800 total. | Common Pitfall: Giving 300 or 600 for homozygous violet instead of 200.', 'HARD', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000fa', 'Two thin lenses of power +3.5 D and -1.5 D are placed in contact. The focal length of the combination is:', '{"A":"+50 cm","B":"+20 cm","C":"-50 cm","D":"+2.0 m"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Light', 1, 'Option (A) is correct. Net power P = P₁ + P₂ = +3.5 - 1.5 = +2.0 D. Focal length f = 1/P = 1/2.0 m = +0.5 m = +50 cm.', 'P = +2.0 D and f = 1/P = +0.5 m = +50 cm [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Convert metres to cm carefully (1 m = 100 cm). | Common Pitfall: Leaving answer in metres or forgetting algebraic sign.', 'EASY', true),
        ('10011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000fb', 'Tooth enamel made of calcium hydroxyapatite starts corroding when the pH in the mouth falls below:', '{"A":"7.0","B":"6.5","C":"5.5","D":"8.2"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Science', 'Acids, Bases and Salts', 1, 'Option (C) is correct. Bacteria produce acids by degrading leftover food/sugars; when mouth pH falls below 5.5, tooth decay starts.', 'State critical pH threshold < 5.5 [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Remember key NCERT pH numbers: Rain < 5.6 (acid rain), Mouth < 5.5 (tooth decay), Soil ~ 7. | Common Pitfall: Confusing acid rain threshold (5.6) with tooth decay (5.5).', 'EASY', true)
